WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:11.353
- So hello, welcome.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.670
My name is Raffaella Zanuttini.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:18.343
I'm a faculty member here in
the Linguistics Department.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.390
When I started planning this event

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:25.530
with the help of some of our students,

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.253
I had two goals in mind.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:33.010
I thought we wanted to
learn more about ASL

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.260
and about sign languages in general:

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.653
their structure, their
history, their importance.

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.390
And we also wanted to celebrate the fact

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.383
that now ASL is a
subject of study at Yale.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.630
So students can learn the language

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.883
and be exposed to issues
relevant to the deaf community.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.150
But as we organized the event

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:57.320
and got in touch with a
number of people at Yale,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.850
we also realized that this gave us

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.860
the opportunity to do something else,

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.330
to meet members of the deaf community

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:07.630
at Yale and beyond Yale,

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.980
and to give them an opportunity
to meet one another.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.450
This is wonderful because it shows that

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:18.450
introducing ASL at Yale is not
only interesting and useful,

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.220
but it also helps us create

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.220
a more inclusive and diverse community,

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:27.023
and this is something
that enriches all of us.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.230
So I'm happy to see you all here.

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.370
I'd like to welcome all of you,

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.533
our panelists and our interpreters.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:36.500
And we're going to start

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.220
with some opening
remarks from three people

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:42.070
who represent three
different groups at Yale:

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:45.530
administration, staff, and faculty.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:47.970
So the first one is Dean George Levesque

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:52.743
from the Yale College Dean's Office.

00:01:57.711 --> 00:02:00.711
(audience applause)

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:03.430
- Thank you very much.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.971
I really just have a few words of welcome

00:02:05.971 --> 00:02:07.880
that I wanna extend

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.020
on behalf of the Yale
College Dean's Office.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.520
I know that we have many
people from, oh, sorry.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:14.353
Is this better?

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:19.310
We have, I know, many people
from across the university,

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:21.580
you are here today, and I
understand we have some colleagues

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.560
from other institutions, too
who have joined us on campus.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.780
Thank you for coming and
helping to be a part of this.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.250
A special thanks to our presenters

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:36.150
who have prepared our
program for this afternoon.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.510
I also wanna take this moment to

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.490
publicly thank a number
of critical partners

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:45.728
across the campus who
have been instrumental,

00:02:45.728 --> 00:02:48.120
as Professor Zanuttini mentioned,

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:52.260
of bringing ASL into the
Yale College curriculum.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.860
I wanna begin by thanking
Professor Zanuttini,

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.140
who as Director of Undergraduate
Studies of Linguistics

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:04.000
was a persistent and eloquent champion

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.270
for ASL to come into the curriculum.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:10.660
Professor Frank is Chair of Linguistics.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.400
It was critically important
to us, in the Dean's Office,

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.460
that ASL have a departmental home

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.260
within the Faculty of Arts and Sciences,

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:22.490
and so it has been very
important for linguistics

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:26.270
to provide the departmental
home and structure for ASL,

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.220
so we thank you for that.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.777
I thank Jessica Tanner,

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:33.130
who has taught ASL through

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:36.450
our Directed Independent
Language Study program,

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.320
our DILS program.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:39.650
You may hear about that
a little bit later.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.480
We have been teaching ASL informally

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.110
through that program
for a number of years,

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:47.210
and Jessica has been very
instrumental in that,

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:50.740
in developing a loyal
following of students.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.330
And I also wanna thank my colleague

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:54.030
who couldn't be here today, I'm sorry,

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.220
Nelleke Van Deusen-Scholl,

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.740
who as Director of the
Center for Language Study,

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.260
supports DILS and funds that program,

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:06.480
so I thank her in her absence.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.440
And lastly, a few more
people if you don't mind,

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:11.610
my colleagues on the
Language Study Committee

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:14.600
and in the Teaching
Resource Advisory Committee.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.160
When we received the
proposal from linguistics,

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.540
it was immediately and
enthusiastically supported

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:22.620
by the members of the committee.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.500
It was really heartening to see faculty

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.440
from across a number of departments
immediately jump onboard

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:34.410
as this is an opportunity to
bring ASL into the curriculum.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:37.520
And none of this, it would
not have been possible

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:38.700
without the support of

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.830
the Dean of the Faculty of Arts
and Sciences, Tamar Gendler,

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:44.050
who received the recommendation

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.970
from the Language Study Committee

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:49.230
and the Teaching Resource
Advisory Committee,

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:54.190
and she also warmly received
it and found funding for it,

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:57.210
for the inaugural teaching position,

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.570
so we thank her for that support.

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:01.903
And last but not least,

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:06.844
I wanna thank the past
and current students

00:05:06.844 --> 00:05:10.210
who have expressed this abiding interest

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:13.463
in studying ASL for a variety of reasons.

00:05:13.463 --> 00:05:14.900
It goes without saying

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.040
that we wouldn't be able
to offer courses in ASL

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.040
if there are no students who
are interested in taking them,

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:24.160
and this has been a wild success.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:28.020
To see the number of students
in our opening sections of ASL

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.470
bursting at the seams
has been very gratifying,

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.340
not only to prepare them, I'm sure,

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:38.181
as citizens in a more diverse
world, but just the vital

00:05:38.181 --> 00:05:43.181
and robust intellectual
field of study that ASL is.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:45.220
And so we're very happy

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:46.740
on behalf of the Yale
College Dean's Office,

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.300
on behalf of Yale College,
to welcome you all here.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.810
Thank you for coming,
and enjoy the afternoon.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:53.869
Thank you.

00:05:53.869 --> 00:05:56.869
(audience applause)

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:04.780
- The next speaker giving
some welcoming remarks

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:06.283
is Cindy Greenspun.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:09.450
- Thank you for having me here.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:10.950
My name is Cindy Greenspun,

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.807
I've worked at Yale for 20 years.

00:06:13.807 --> 00:06:16.563
I work for the Yale Library system.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:19.410
I've been deaf all of my life,

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:22.220
I lost my hearing when
I was two years old.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:25.350
I was mainstreamed in the
public school system in Austin,

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:26.740
I was the only deaf person

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:29.063
in those schools that I've attended.

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:32.770
It was a struggle to keep
up with my classmates,

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:35.973
and my grades were nothing to be proud of.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:41.780
I made it to college and my
first year was also a struggle

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:45.850
until I realized that if
I learned sign language,

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:48.835
I might get the help and
the support that I need

00:06:48.835 --> 00:06:50.520
to keep up with my classmates.

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:54.726
So I made it a goal to
learn sign language, and

00:06:54.726 --> 00:06:59.030
the next semester, I'm happy
to report to all of you

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:01.950
that for the rest of my
semesters in college,

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:02.850
my grades were As.

00:07:04.918 --> 00:07:06.818
(audience applauds)
I was very proud.

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:12.553
American Sign Language is amazing.

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:14.970
What's even more amazing,

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:18.070
and I'm really grateful it
got American Sign Language

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.143
as part of the curriculum at Yale.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:24.460
And I hope that it's as useful,

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:27.672
as helpful to others

00:07:27.672 --> 00:07:30.500
who might be in the same
sort of situation as I,

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:33.773
or to anybody who's hearing or not.

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:37.600
So thank you for all of
your hard work to everybody,

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:41.120
to the Yale community who
have bring sign language

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.840
to the university.

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:44.084
Thank you.

00:07:44.084 --> 00:07:47.084
(audience applauds)

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:55.700
- Bob Frank, who's the chair
of the linguistics department.

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:04.450
- So I would like to welcome
everybody who's here,

00:08:05.452 --> 00:08:10.360
and thank our speakers
for coming and for sharing

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:13.320
what I'm sure will be a
fascinating afternoon.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.850
I'd also like to echo the thanks

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:18.160
that Dean Levesque made earlier,

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.640
which actually I have
exactly the same individuals

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:21.910
listed on my paper in front of me

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:24.620
but I will not repeat them for you,

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:27.030
but thank you to everybody
who's been involved

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:31.300
in this extremely vital
and important initiative

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:36.300
in increasing diversity at
Yale in a very important way

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:40.161
by incorporating ASL and deaf culture

00:08:40.161 --> 00:08:43.940
to studies at Yale University.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:46.570
So, one of the things that
Dean Levesque mentioned

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:48.400
was that it was really crucial

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.050
in bringing ASL studies to Yale

00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:54.590
was for ASL to have a departmental home,

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:57.690
and the question of
where that should be was,

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:00.220
in a sense, for linguists,
an easy question.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.430
That we were very, very enthusiastic,

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.840
all of our colleagues were enthusiastic

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:06.630
about being that home

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:09.660
because of the importance of
the study of sign language

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.683
to the study of language in general.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:16.130
From the perspective of understanding

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:19.140
the nature of linguistic structure,

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:24.140
the study of sign language is
an important domain of inquiry

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:27.470
because one is tempted to try to relate

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:31.000
the nature of language to
the fact that it is spoken,

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.690
and so one tries to derive, in part,

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:34.880
some of the linguistic structure

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:36.770
from the constraints that are imposed

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:40.087
by the need to speak or the need to hear.

00:09:40.087 --> 00:09:43.987
And sign language poses
an interesting alternative

00:09:43.987 --> 00:09:47.830
in that the different
modality that is used

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:52.830
for its speaking and perception
raises other kinds of issues

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:54.740
and raises the question of,

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:56.810
well, is sign language just fundamentally

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:58.220
of a different sort?

00:09:58.220 --> 00:09:59.750
And what has been discovered

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:02.850
in the work over the last
almost half-century now

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:04.870
has been that essentially no,

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:06.950
that what we learned from sign language

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:09.510
is that there's
fundamentally a commonality

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:12.040
in human experience, in the human mind,

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.760
that tells us that sign
language is a language,

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:18.955
and its structure and its
underlying cognitive mechanisms

00:10:18.955 --> 00:10:23.170
are the same as those
involved in spoken language.

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:26.050
And that's been profoundly important,

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:27.670
I think, both within linguistics,

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:31.000
but also in understanding
our shared humanity.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.091
The second area in which sign language

00:10:34.091 --> 00:10:37.783
has been scientifically
significant comes about

00:10:37.783 --> 00:10:40.790
as the result of some distinctive issues

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:44.820
that arise in the experience
of the deaf community.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:47.780
Some of these are
extremely exciting issues,

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:51.583
other things are unfortunate
in the deaf experience.

00:10:51.583 --> 00:10:55.200
So because of the unique
experience of many deaf individuals

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:58.830
not being exposed to sign
language until later in life,

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:03.830
this allows the study
of unique experiences

00:11:03.867 --> 00:11:07.860
that are characteristic of
what happens to individuals

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:10.340
when they're not exposed to a
language until later in life,

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:12.250
and how does that impact

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:14.360
the way in which they learn language?

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:16.720
So these kinds of studies
have been extremely important

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:18.751
and have been very enlightening.

00:11:18.751 --> 00:11:21.400
In fact, our first speaker,
so I will now transition,

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:23.500
our first speaker today will be telling us

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:26.300
something about this
kind of unique experience

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:28.870
that arises in sign language.

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:31.700
In this case, in the domain
of Nicaraguan Sign Language.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:34.720
So our first speaker, Annemarie Kocab,

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:38.373
got her Ph.D. from Harvard
in Psychology in 2017.

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:42.665
She is now, continues
at Harvard as a PostDoc

00:11:42.665 --> 00:11:45.620
working jointly with Jesse Snedeker,

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:47.690
who's a professor in the
Psychology Department,

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:50.540
and our own former PostDoc, Kate Davidson,

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:53.053
now of the Harvard Linguistics Department.

00:11:53.990 --> 00:11:57.269
Annemarie's work is a
sort of remarkable example

00:11:57.269 --> 00:12:00.706
of someone who combines,
in an exciting way,

00:12:00.706 --> 00:12:05.706
a careful and refined understanding
of linguistic structure

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:09.770
and of the dimensions of variation

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:13.360
that inform what goes
into linguistic structure

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:15.440
together with a profound understanding

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:17.107
of the cognitive foundations and

00:12:17.107 --> 00:12:19.210
the cognitive factors that are involved

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:23.510
in language learning
and in language change.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:24.960
Much of her work has studied

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:28.913
the unique experience
of signers in Nicaragua,

00:12:28.913 --> 00:12:31.810
where a new sign language
has come into being

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:35.403
in the recent past, and
so she has focused on

00:12:35.403 --> 00:12:40.076
this case study of language
emergence over the short term,

00:12:40.076 --> 00:12:43.870
looking at a variety of
questions from referential shift,

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:45.560
to the formation of questions,

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:47.920
to the existence of recursive structures.

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:49.940
She's also looked at a
variety of other factors,

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:52.910
including the representation of telicity

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:57.020
and the use of non-manual
markers and iconicity.

00:12:57.020 --> 00:12:58.550
So it's a real great pleasure for us

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:01.680
to have Dr. Kocab join us

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:04.980
and give us our first
talk today, so welcome.

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:07.980
(audience applause)

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:21.380
- [Interpreter] Thank you for
that wonderful introduction,

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:22.910
and thank you for having me here.

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:24.320
It's a very exciting event,

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:26.983
and thank the organizers
for planning all of this.

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:31.780
Language is universal and ubiquitous.

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:34.670
All human societies in
the World have a language.

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:37.173
The question is, where
does language come from?

00:13:39.830 --> 00:13:41.950
There are two kinds of explanations

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:44.100
that are not mutually exclusive.

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:45.920
One is that the structure of language

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:47.850
comes from biological evolution

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:50.730
and reflects properties of
our cognitive architecture,

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.333
how our brains have evolved.

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:55.410
The second is that language reflects

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:58.260
a product of cultural evolution,

00:13:58.260 --> 00:13:59.760
so the kind of human capacity

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:02.523
for social learning and
cultural transmission.

00:14:06.382 --> 00:14:07.504
Uh-oh.

00:14:07.504 --> 00:14:10.504
(audience laughter)

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:15.500
Here we have a timeline

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:19.810
that helps us to understand
these explanations.

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:22.460
The dotted line in the
center represents the time

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:25.570
when Homo sapiens dispersed from Africa

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:28.740
around at least 70,000 years ago.

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:31.290
Because all humans have language,

00:14:31.290 --> 00:14:34.110
any mechanism that allows
us to acquire language

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:37.403
must have been in place before
we migrated out of Africa.

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:43.760
On the first explanation,

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:44.920
where the structure of language

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:46.630
comes from biological evolution,

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:48.660
we have a timeline like this.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:51.780
On the y-axis we have
linguistic resources,

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:54.359
and on the x-axis we have time.

00:14:54.359 --> 00:14:57.970
On this position, over thousands of years,

00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:00.080
evolution selects for adaptations

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:02.550
that allow for human language creation,

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:04.430
meaning any individual mind

00:15:04.430 --> 00:15:07.013
is capable of creating
and acquiring language.

00:15:11.149 --> 00:15:12.825
On the other extreme,

00:15:12.825 --> 00:15:16.030
language comes from cultural processes,

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:18.900
similar to how Mathematics
and Science were constructed

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:20.730
over thousands and thousands of years

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:23.240
with each generation building on the work

00:15:23.240 --> 00:15:24.713
of the previous generation.

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:31.390
So getting data to help
us address the question

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:36.020
of biological versus cultural
evolution is difficult,

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:37.900
and most languages have been around

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:39.410
for hundreds of thousands of years,

00:15:39.410 --> 00:15:43.020
and their patterns of emergence
are largely lost to time.

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:44.520
But we do have several tools

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:46.183
to help us get at this question,

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:51.460
one of which is the study
of emerging languages.

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:52.920
In this talk, I'll be talking about

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:55.253
one particular language in Nicaragua.

00:15:56.840 --> 00:15:59.280
If you go to Nicaragua
today, you'll see a community

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:01.210
that looks like many other
language communities.

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:02.480
If you go to a school for the deaf,

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:04.310
you'll see the children
there are hanging out,

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:07.380
playing and interacting
all in sign language.

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:10.113
That language didn't exist 40 years ago.

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:12.810
Because it emerged so recently,

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:15.820
the people who created the
language are still alive today

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:17.910
and we can study them to better understand

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:19.343
where language comes from.

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:25.140
Let me tell you a little bit

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:27.610
about the history of
Nicaraguan Sign Language.

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:29.190
You may know that most deaf children

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:32.380
are born to hearing parents
who do not know sign language,

00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:34.350
meaning that they have
no accessible language

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:35.900
in their environment.

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:39.393
That was the situation in
Nicaragua until the 1970s.

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:45.760
In those situations, deaf
individuals are propelled

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:48.100
by a social desire to
communicate with other people,

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:52.070
and they'll create gestural
systems called Home Sign Systems

00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:54.410
to use with their family members.

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:56.570
These systems are internal to the family,

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:58.520
they're not used outside of the family.

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:04.670
The situation in Nicaragua
changed drastically in 1970s

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:05.970
when the government established

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:07.760
a school for Special Education

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:10.090
that pulled together deaf
children in large numbers

00:17:10.090 --> 00:17:11.623
for the first time.

00:17:13.840 --> 00:17:15.340
They did not use sign language

00:17:15.340 --> 00:17:16.760
as a method of instruction in school,

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:19.480
they used oral methods, but
the children brought with them

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:21.210
their individual home sign systems

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:23.220
and began to gesture to
communicate with each other

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:24.800
on the playground, in the bus,

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:28.053
and at that point, language
began to emerge and develop.

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:31.540
We call the first group of
children to enter the school

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:32.973
the first cohort.

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:37.720
What allowed the language
to continue to grow

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:40.290
is that another group of children,

00:17:40.290 --> 00:17:42.420
additional groups of children

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:43.620
continued to enter the school

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:46.770
and learn their language from
the first group of children.

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:48.370
For the purposes of comparing

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:50.760
how the language has changed over time,

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:53.100
we've divided these children into groups

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:55.333
of roughly 10-year increments.

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:58.550
The second group is the second cohort,

00:17:58.550 --> 00:18:00.600
and then the third cohort came in.

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:03.490
Each cohort represents
a successive time point

00:18:03.490 --> 00:18:05.240
in the development of the language.

00:18:09.710 --> 00:18:12.120
Not all deaf individuals
enter the deaf community.

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:15.950
There are many who never learn
a sign or spoken language,

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:17.920
so their Home Sign Systems remain

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:20.520
their primary mode of communication.

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.810
These systems represent
the kinds of systems

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:25.433
that individuals in
isolation come up with.

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:33.210
I'm going to show you brief snapshots

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:35.290
of what Nicaraguan Sign
Language looks like.

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:36.960
I know you don't know NSL,

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:39.640
and I'll be telling you what
to look for in each clip.

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:41.030
The first video that I show you

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:43.690
will be the language of the
first cohort of signers.

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:48.040
You'll see that their signs
are larger, slower, measured,

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:50.383
and there's not a lot
of rapid turn-taking.

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:17.770
Now I'll show you the
language of the second cohort.

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:19.260
These signers as children

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:21.450
learned their language
from the first cohort,

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:23.100
and you'll see that their
language looks different.

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:25.643
It's faster, there's
more rapid turn-taking.

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:43.960
And now I'll show you a
video of the Cohort 3 signers

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:46.350
who learned their language
from the second cohort.

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:47.750
Their language looks very different.

00:19:47.750 --> 00:19:48.770
It's even faster,

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:51.760
there's a lot of simultaneous
communication going on,

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:53.183
very interactive.

00:20:13.990 --> 00:20:17.750
As you might suspect, biology
alone and culture alone

00:20:17.750 --> 00:20:20.190
can't fully explain what we've seen so far

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:22.853
with the first three
cohorts of NSL signers.

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:25.940
It seems that language does not take

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:28.280
hundreds of thousands of years to develop,

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:30.360
but also it does not arise immediately

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:32.383
in the first generation fully formed.

00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:37.640
We see fast emergence of words

00:20:37.640 --> 00:20:40.280
and the linguistic capacity
to express propositions,

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:42.663
so the story is partly biological.

00:20:46.360 --> 00:20:48.680
But as this research program has developed

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:50.420
over the last 20 to 30 years,

00:20:50.420 --> 00:20:52.510
we found that some linguistic structures

00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:55.700
take additional time to develop.

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:58.990
For example, the marking
of argument structure,

00:20:58.990 --> 00:21:01.283
which indicates who did what to whom.

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:06.250
These intergenerational
processes happen rather quickly

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:10.500
in contrast to the typical
construal of cultural processes

00:21:10.500 --> 00:21:12.073
as long and protracted ones.

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:18.590
Therefore, we have a third alternative.

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:21.710
The pattern of language
emergence looks rather like this,

00:21:21.710 --> 00:21:23.820
where we have contributions from biology

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:26.630
that equip our minds to create language

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:31.850
with cultural interaction

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:35.653
that allows for further
linguistic devices to develop.

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:41.033
Let's zoom in for a moment.

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:48.720
On the x-axis we have
the home signers here

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:50.490
because these are systems that represent

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:53.320
the kind of systems that
individuals who come up with these

00:21:53.320 --> 00:21:57.360
as a consequence of biology
and living in human society.

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:00.223
The first three cohorts are placed here.

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:07.240
These boxes are some of the
things that we've seen develop

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:08.880
as NSL has developed and changed.

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:11.330
I'm not gonna talk about
all of these things,

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:12.970
but you do see that some things come up

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:14.120
in Home Sign Systems,

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:16.440
some emerge in the first cohort of signers

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:17.970
as soon as the community forms,

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:20.463
and other things take
more time to develop.

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:27.950
In today's talk, I'm gonna talk
about two specific projects

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:29.650
in which I've looked at
the linguistic structure

00:22:29.650 --> 00:22:32.863
of NSL, recursion, and quantifiers.

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:36.720
These case studies show
how studying sign language

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:39.040
can help us understand
more about language itself

00:22:39.040 --> 00:22:39.990
and how it emerges.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:44.840
These cases are interesting to linguists

00:22:44.840 --> 00:22:46.970
because these are places where the mapping

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:50.693
between syntax and
semantics becomes complex.

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:55.760
The first project I'm going to look at

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:59.780
is the historical evolution of recursion,

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:04.653
which is proposed to be a
property of all human languages.

00:23:09.833 --> 00:23:12.860
You can think about
recursion at two levels.

00:23:12.860 --> 00:23:16.160
You take the sentence, John
knows Mary knows Bill lied.

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:21.160
On the meaning side, we have
a knowledge state, John's,

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:25.450
with another knowledge state
embedded in it, Mary's,

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:29.033
with a third event, Bill's
lying, embedded in that.

00:23:31.660 --> 00:23:33.280
On the level of syntax,

00:23:33.280 --> 00:23:35.330
we have a simplified phrase structure

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:36.960
with a clause embedded within a clause

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:38.923
with yet another clause embedded in it.

00:23:40.220 --> 00:23:42.670
So recursion is argued to allow

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:46.253
language to have discrete infinity,

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:51.020
where you could compose an
infinite number of utterances

00:23:51.020 --> 00:23:53.563
with a finite set of words and rules.

00:23:57.870 --> 00:24:01.380
Recursion is often thought to
be universal and ubiquitous,

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:03.130
and some have made the strong claim

00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:05.680
that it's the only thing
that separates human language

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:07.910
from animal communication systems.

00:24:07.910 --> 00:24:11.340
At least one language has
been argued to lack recursion,

00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:13.093
Pirahaã in the Amazon.

00:24:15.690 --> 00:24:18.180
Instead of producing embedded structures,

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:21.800
Pirahaã is argued to use parataxis.

00:24:21.800 --> 00:24:25.110
So instead of saying something
like John's sister's house,

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:27.430
which is embedded in English,

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:28.330
they would instead produce

00:24:28.330 --> 00:24:30.380
a flat structure with no embedding.

00:24:30.380 --> 00:24:31.880
John has a sister.

00:24:31.880 --> 00:24:33.513
That sister has a house.

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:36.940
This claim is debated.

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:38.280
Part of the difficulty

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:42.220
is the difficulty of
interpreting a lack of evidence,

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:44.690
and also there are differences in opinion

00:24:44.690 --> 00:24:48.560
on how the language is analyzed,
so we're at an impasse.

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:52.290
Is recursion a fundamental
property of human language?

00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:55.192
We designed studies to go in and analyze

00:24:55.192 --> 00:24:57.977
Nicaraguan Sign Language signers

00:24:57.977 --> 00:24:59.773
and home signers to find out.

00:25:05.719 --> 00:25:09.040
We looked at if recursive
structures are available

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:12.480
to specify a previously
mentioned character,

00:25:12.480 --> 00:25:14.160
to pick them out from a set

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:15.500
of previously mentioned characters,

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:17.510
to pick out a specific individual

00:25:17.510 --> 00:25:20.600
from a previously mentioned
set of characters.

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:25.060
So that function is often
accomplished in language

00:25:25.060 --> 00:25:27.033
through the use of relative clauses,

00:25:28.290 --> 00:25:30.863
where you have a clause
embedded in another clause.

00:25:32.340 --> 00:25:35.260
We designed events and showed
movies to our participants.

00:25:35.260 --> 00:25:37.840
I'm using still pictures
for the sake of time.

00:25:37.840 --> 00:25:41.373
The first condition was designed
to elicit relative clauses.

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:45.420
You have a set of girls, each
doing something different,

00:25:45.420 --> 00:25:47.833
and then one of the
girls does something new.

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:51.310
The target description
will be something like

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:53.773
the girl who was drawing
removed the picture.

00:25:59.950 --> 00:26:01.790
The second condition was designed

00:26:01.790 --> 00:26:04.110
to elicit conjoined clauses.

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:06.136
By removing the other two characters,

00:26:06.136 --> 00:26:08.105
we expect to elicit descriptions

00:26:08.105 --> 00:26:10.870
with a flat structure without embedding.

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:13.053
A girl drew and then removed the picture.

00:26:17.132 --> 00:26:20.070
The third condition had the same character

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:21.960
performing the same action twice,

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:23.350
drawing on a piece of paper

00:26:23.350 --> 00:26:25.000
and then drawing on an easel pad.

00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:28.090
Relative clauses are not the only way

00:26:28.090 --> 00:26:29.473
to describe these stimuli.

00:26:30.950 --> 00:26:33.730
A person could use parataxis instead

00:26:33.730 --> 00:26:37.111
where they mention the
girl and the action twice.

00:26:37.111 --> 00:26:38.431
The girl drew.

00:26:38.431 --> 00:26:40.810
The girl removed the picture.

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:42.790
That's the kind of structure
that we would expect

00:26:42.790 --> 00:26:45.080
if the language lacks recursion,

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:46.573
like what we see in Pirahã.

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:54.560
We predict that if the
language has recursion,

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:57.960
then the ability to convey
embedded messages would be there.

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:01.340
We expect them to produce utterances

00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:03.130
that have the meanings of relative clauses

00:27:03.130 --> 00:27:04.740
to establish a set of characters,

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:07.010
and then pick out an
individual from that set

00:27:07.010 --> 00:27:09.683
and predicate something
new about that individual.

00:27:11.270 --> 00:27:14.610
On the syntax side,
prior observation of NSL

00:27:14.610 --> 00:27:18.393
allows us to predict what
relative clauses might look like.

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:23.300
Many sign languages do not
use complementizers, like who,

00:27:23.300 --> 00:27:24.960
to mark relative clauses.

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:29.340
But verbs in sign language
can make morphological changes

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:31.470
in size and duration and motion.

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:34.410
So we expect that the
verb, when it's embedded,

00:27:34.410 --> 00:27:37.030
will be shorter and
faster compared to verbs

00:27:37.030 --> 00:27:39.190
that occur in non-embedded contexts,

00:27:39.190 --> 00:27:42.053
providing specific visual
evidence of embedding.

00:27:45.350 --> 00:27:49.010
I'm gonna show a video of a
signer who does exactly that.

00:27:49.010 --> 00:27:51.200
You'll see that she
first establishes a set,

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:53.600
picks out one person, and
then says something new

00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:56.690
about that person, and
when she describes that,

00:27:56.690 --> 00:27:57.980
she repeats the verb.

00:27:57.980 --> 00:28:00.340
It's smaller and faster
than the first time

00:28:00.340 --> 00:28:01.750
that she describes it.

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:03.610
I'll show you the videotape.

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:07.093
It's slowed down, and it has
glosses for your convenience.

00:28:30.340 --> 00:28:31.843
I'm gonna play it again.

00:28:31.843 --> 00:28:33.080
I want everyone to see

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:35.470
that eating the apple the
second time was shorter,

00:28:35.470 --> 00:28:36.843
so let's watch it again.

00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:11.400
That kind of description is
our candidate relative clause,

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:12.860
where signers established a set

00:29:12.860 --> 00:29:14.940
and then pick out an individual.

00:29:14.940 --> 00:29:18.060
The underlined section
specifically is our relative clause

00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:19.253
with the repeated verb.

00:29:20.420 --> 00:29:22.982
We saw signers from all three cohorts

00:29:22.982 --> 00:29:25.380
produce those kind of repeated strings

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:27.160
on the relative clause trials,

00:29:27.160 --> 00:29:29.133
but not in the other two conditions.

00:29:30.170 --> 00:29:32.320
We interpreted this as a relative clause

00:29:32.320 --> 00:29:35.310
rather than parataxis for two reasons.

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:38.300
The first is that in theory,

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:43.300
that string could be
considered as conjunction.

00:29:43.980 --> 00:29:47.910
But that interpretation is
unlikely in this context

00:29:47.910 --> 00:29:49.540
because the verb, eating an apple,

00:29:49.540 --> 00:29:51.930
has already been completed, and
it's already been mentioned,

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:54.080
and therefore it's
relevant only to the extent

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:57.080
to which it identifies
the girl under discussion.

00:29:57.080 --> 00:29:58.500
And the second reason

00:29:58.500 --> 00:30:01.200
is because the verb itself looks different

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:02.880
when it's embedded in a relative clause

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:05.480
as opposed to when it appears
in a conjoined clause.

00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:11.340
We looked at the form of the
verb in the two conditions.

00:30:13.024 --> 00:30:15.810
To quantify this, we took the ratio

00:30:15.810 --> 00:30:18.535
of the length of the first verb, draw,

00:30:18.535 --> 00:30:21.270
to the second verb, remove the picture,

00:30:21.270 --> 00:30:25.620
and what we found is that
the verb draw is shorter

00:30:25.620 --> 00:30:28.170
when it's in the relative clause.

00:30:28.170 --> 00:30:30.500
As you can see by the smaller purple bar

00:30:30.500 --> 00:30:32.980
compared to when it appears
in the conjoined clause,

00:30:32.980 --> 00:30:34.930
where you see by the longer purple bar.

00:30:38.885 --> 00:30:40.980
So we suspect that the shortening

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:44.203
is a morpho-syntactic
way of marking embedding.

00:30:47.590 --> 00:30:49.670
To make sure that the
shortening isn't happening

00:30:49.670 --> 00:30:51.982
anytime the same verb is repeated,

00:30:51.982 --> 00:30:55.440
we looked at the repeated action condition

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:56.870
where we had the same person

00:30:56.870 --> 00:30:59.760
doing the same action
twice, drawing and drawing,

00:30:59.760 --> 00:31:02.320
and we do not see any
shortening of the verb there,

00:31:02.320 --> 00:31:05.600
so it seems the shortening
specifically occurs

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:06.800
when a verb is embedded.

00:31:11.978 --> 00:31:15.153
So that raises the question,

00:31:15.153 --> 00:31:17.800
can individuals who have not been part

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:20.270
of a language community
produce relative clauses?

00:31:20.270 --> 00:31:23.030
We tested adult home signers in Nicaragua,

00:31:23.030 --> 00:31:24.720
and I would remind you
that these are individuals

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:26.920
who have never learned a
sign or spoken language,

00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:28.580
and their home sign systems

00:31:28.580 --> 00:31:30.730
remain their primary
mode of communication.

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:36.760
We tested four adult
Nicaraguan home signers,

00:31:36.760 --> 00:31:40.000
and first we checked to make
sure they understood the task,

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:42.140
to see if they described
the target character

00:31:42.140 --> 00:31:44.613
and the critical action, and they all did.

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:51.180
When we look at the descriptions
in the different conditions

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:52.650
we see a different pattern

00:31:52.650 --> 00:31:55.053
compared to the Nicaraguan
Sign Language users.

00:31:56.180 --> 00:31:58.002
On the relative clause trials,

00:31:58.002 --> 00:32:00.720
the home signers did not introduce the set

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:03.430
or repeat the identifying verb.

00:32:03.430 --> 00:32:06.210
In fact, their descriptions
looked very similar

00:32:06.210 --> 00:32:08.470
to what we see in the
conjoined clause descriptions,

00:32:08.470 --> 00:32:10.290
where they describe the target character

00:32:10.290 --> 00:32:11.523
and the new action only.

00:32:13.630 --> 00:32:15.350
The verbs don't look any different

00:32:15.350 --> 00:32:16.700
in these conditions either.

00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:22.880
So there are three possible
interpretations of this finding.

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:24.910
The first is that perhaps home signers

00:32:24.910 --> 00:32:26.850
are producing embedded verbs,

00:32:26.850 --> 00:32:29.650
but they're not marking
them with the shortening.

00:32:29.650 --> 00:32:31.140
The second possibility is maybe

00:32:31.140 --> 00:32:35.820
that home signers can't conceive
of recursive expressions.

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:37.100
The third possibility is

00:32:37.100 --> 00:32:40.150
that home signers have recursion in mind,

00:32:40.150 --> 00:32:44.270
but they're using parataxis
to communicate that.

00:32:44.270 --> 00:32:46.910
Based on prior work on
the kinds of messages

00:32:46.910 --> 00:32:48.900
that home signers are able to convey,

00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:52.570
we suspect that the third
hypothesis is the correct one.

00:32:52.570 --> 00:32:55.120
So that home signers do
have recursion in mind,

00:32:55.120 --> 00:32:57.980
but they produce paratactic utterances,

00:32:57.980 --> 00:33:00.000
similar to what we see in other languages

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:02.523
that are argued to lack
recursion, like Pirahã.

00:33:08.870 --> 00:33:11.730
So we see that the basic
ability to produce recursion

00:33:11.730 --> 00:33:14.850
is present very early in the
emergence of a new language.

00:33:14.850 --> 00:33:18.490
All NSL signers across all
three cohorts differentiate

00:33:18.490 --> 00:33:20.650
between embedded and
non-embedded contexts.

00:33:20.650 --> 00:33:22.655
Home signers do not.

00:33:22.655 --> 00:33:24.993
They may be using parataxis.

00:33:26.830 --> 00:33:30.160
So what allows recursion that's present,

00:33:30.160 --> 00:33:34.440
to be present in Cohort 1
signers but not in home signers?

00:33:34.440 --> 00:33:36.530
A key difference between those two groups

00:33:36.530 --> 00:33:40.150
is that Cohort 1 signers are
part of a language community.

00:33:40.150 --> 00:33:42.880
They have the opportunity to
interact with their peers,

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:46.810
and to use their peers'
utterances as input to their own.

00:33:46.810 --> 00:33:49.410
So it's possible that
the home sign messages

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:54.410
that had embedded meaning
became input to deaf children,

00:33:54.730 --> 00:33:56.640
who then reanalyzed the language

00:33:56.640 --> 00:33:59.063
to create new linguistic structures.

00:34:01.700 --> 00:34:03.550
Now I'm gonna move on to the second part,

00:34:03.550 --> 00:34:06.733
or the second project,
looking at quantifiers.

00:34:10.919 --> 00:34:12.806
Quantifiers in natural languages express

00:34:12.806 --> 00:34:15.883
the range of properties between,

00:34:17.110 --> 00:34:19.610
the relationships between properties,

00:34:19.610 --> 00:34:21.533
like some birds are flying.

00:34:23.640 --> 00:34:27.190
Quantifiers allow us to
form generalizations,

00:34:27.190 --> 00:34:29.583
like most bananas are clones.

00:34:31.747 --> 00:34:35.370
Most, maybe all languages
do have quantifiers,

00:34:35.370 --> 00:34:38.723
so maybe there's something
special about quantifiers

00:34:38.723 --> 00:34:41.370
that can help us understand
more about human cognition.

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:43.040
We tested this by looking at quantifiers

00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:45.230
in the first three cohorts

00:34:45.230 --> 00:34:47.053
of Nicaraguan Sign Language users.

00:34:53.610 --> 00:34:57.020
As I said, quantifiers
express the relationship

00:34:57.020 --> 00:34:58.960
between two properties,

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:02.200
so we elicited quantifiers
by showing signers pictures

00:35:02.200 --> 00:35:06.243
with large sets of individuals like bears.

00:35:07.290 --> 00:35:09.833
Then we showed them two paired pictures.

00:35:10.930 --> 00:35:15.020
One is a subset where a subset is engaging

00:35:15.020 --> 00:35:17.940
in an entirely new activity,
in this case swimming,

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:20.963
and the difference is
the size of the subset.

00:35:22.660 --> 00:35:24.862
So we would expect something like

00:35:24.862 --> 00:35:27.340
some of the bears are swimming,

00:35:27.340 --> 00:35:30.090
and here we would expect all
of the bears are swimming.

00:35:33.900 --> 00:35:34.733
Here's another example

00:35:34.733 --> 00:35:37.003
intended to elicit different quantifiers.

00:35:38.980 --> 00:35:41.220
By contrast, we would
get descriptions like

00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:42.923
none of the boys climb the tree.

00:35:47.640 --> 00:35:50.473
And here we could get many
of the birds flew away.

00:35:54.870 --> 00:35:57.013
We looked at three types of quantifiers.

00:35:58.030 --> 00:35:59.930
Universal quantifiers,

00:35:59.930 --> 00:36:02.170
which are used when
every member of that set

00:36:02.170 --> 00:36:05.023
satisfies the proposition, all.

00:36:06.346 --> 00:36:09.573
So really, given any
X, property P is true.

00:36:11.350 --> 00:36:14.650
I'm gonna show you an example of a sign

00:36:14.650 --> 00:36:17.263
from Nicaraguan Sign Language for all.

00:36:19.447 --> 00:36:20.363
It's very fast.

00:36:22.797 --> 00:36:24.660
All.

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:26.203
That's their sign for all.

00:36:27.552 --> 00:36:29.700
The second quantifier that we elicited

00:36:29.700 --> 00:36:31.203
were existential quantifiers,

00:36:32.271 --> 00:36:34.530
where the propositional
function holds true

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:37.260
of at least one member of the set.

00:36:37.260 --> 00:36:39.753
Many, some, a few, a little.

00:36:44.240 --> 00:36:47.250
Technically, an existential
quantifier can be used

00:36:47.250 --> 00:36:49.770
in a context that it would be described

00:36:49.770 --> 00:36:50.860
by a universal quantifier.

00:36:50.860 --> 00:36:55.860
For example, if all the
boys climb the tree,

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:58.650
it's also true that some
of the boys climb the tree,

00:36:58.650 --> 00:37:01.460
but that tends to be less
pragmatically preferred

00:37:01.460 --> 00:37:03.160
and the reverse is not true.

00:37:03.160 --> 00:37:06.280
You can't say all of the
boys climbed the tree

00:37:06.280 --> 00:37:07.543
if only some of them did.

00:37:11.300 --> 00:37:13.100
Here's an example of one sign

00:37:13.100 --> 00:37:15.213
for the quantifier many in NSL.

00:37:18.410 --> 00:37:19.243
Many.

00:37:22.860 --> 00:37:24.550
The last quantifier type

00:37:24.550 --> 00:37:26.600
that we elicited is negative quantifiers,

00:37:27.960 --> 00:37:32.960
the negation of an existentially
quantified statement.

00:37:33.900 --> 00:37:37.003
So it's not the case that
member P has property X.

00:37:39.720 --> 00:37:43.193
This is an example of
the sign for none in NSL.

00:37:46.970 --> 00:37:47.803
None.

00:37:51.160 --> 00:37:51.993
For the results,

00:37:51.993 --> 00:37:54.943
I'm gonna start with
existential quantifiers first.

00:37:59.910 --> 00:38:02.780
On the x-axis we have cohort,

00:38:02.780 --> 00:38:05.750
and on the y-axis we have the trials.

00:38:05.750 --> 00:38:09.030
The colors represent
different quantifier types.

00:38:09.030 --> 00:38:11.840
Yellow is for negative
quantifiers like none,

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:14.910
green is for existential
quantifiers like many,

00:38:14.910 --> 00:38:17.557
and blue is for universal
quantifiers like all.

00:38:17.557 --> 00:38:22.010
And what we see here is that
when signers see a picture

00:38:22.010 --> 00:38:25.620
designed to elicit existential
quantifiers like this,

00:38:25.620 --> 00:38:28.040
they produce existential quantifiers.

00:38:28.040 --> 00:38:29.473
Many birds flew away.

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:35.870
Now I'm gonna show you the results

00:38:35.870 --> 00:38:37.880
for the negative quantifiers.

00:38:37.880 --> 00:38:39.790
These were just designed
to elicit things like

00:38:39.790 --> 00:38:41.490
none of the boys climbed the tree.

00:38:45.330 --> 00:38:47.830
The colors mean the same
thing as they did before.

00:38:49.060 --> 00:38:51.240
The yellow bar means
that we see a lot of use

00:38:51.240 --> 00:38:54.840
of negative quantifiers
across all three cohorts.

00:38:54.840 --> 00:38:57.170
We also see some occurrences

00:38:57.170 --> 00:39:00.643
of existential and universal quantifiers.

00:39:00.643 --> 00:39:03.308
When we look more closely at
those types of description,

00:39:03.308 --> 00:39:06.870
they were using them paired
with different predicates.

00:39:06.870 --> 00:39:09.432
Many of the boys were standing,

00:39:09.432 --> 00:39:11.950
instead of none of the
boys climbed the tree,

00:39:11.950 --> 00:39:13.720
which is a perfectly acceptable way

00:39:13.720 --> 00:39:16.150
of describing that
picture, but it's important

00:39:16.150 --> 00:39:18.370
that they do have negative quantifiers

00:39:18.370 --> 00:39:19.420
in all three cohorts.

00:39:22.940 --> 00:39:27.563
Now I'll show the pattern for
universal quantifiers, all.

00:39:33.140 --> 00:39:34.890
We'll see a different pattern here.

00:39:36.350 --> 00:39:38.090
We see that the first cohort signers

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:41.870
tend to use existential
quantifiers in contexts

00:39:41.870 --> 00:39:43.890
that where it's preferential
to use universal.

00:39:43.890 --> 00:39:44.940
So if you see a picture

00:39:44.940 --> 00:39:46.190
where all of the boys climbed the tree,

00:39:46.190 --> 00:39:48.253
they tend to say many boys.

00:39:49.730 --> 00:39:52.040
But the younger signers
from Cohorts 2 and 3

00:39:52.040 --> 00:39:54.370
tend to use more universal quantifiers,

00:39:54.370 --> 00:39:55.993
all the boys climbed the tree.

00:39:58.460 --> 00:40:00.520
The use of the existential quantifier,

00:40:00.520 --> 00:40:02.903
as I mentioned before,
is not strictly wrong.

00:40:04.180 --> 00:40:07.268
It's true that there are many
of the boys climbed the tree,

00:40:07.268 --> 00:40:09.333
but it's under-informative.

00:40:10.520 --> 00:40:12.810
A strong statement would
be better, all the boys.

00:40:12.810 --> 00:40:15.430
For example, if I told you
that I ate some of the cake,

00:40:15.430 --> 00:40:18.967
but in fact I ate all of
it, you might be mad at me.

00:40:18.967 --> 00:40:19.800
(audience laughs)
Because you thought,

00:40:19.800 --> 00:40:20.663
where's my cake?

00:40:23.710 --> 00:40:26.120
One possible interpretation
of this pattern

00:40:26.120 --> 00:40:30.640
is that the pragmatic process
that underlie quantification,

00:40:30.640 --> 00:40:34.959
is something that takes time
to develop in a new language.

00:40:34.959 --> 00:40:37.590
For future work, we'd like
to pursue this further,

00:40:37.590 --> 00:40:40.470
and we also plan to take a
look at this in home signers

00:40:40.470 --> 00:40:42.450
to understand if quantifiers is something

00:40:42.450 --> 00:40:46.430
that people who are not in a
language community can express,

00:40:46.430 --> 00:40:47.263
like...

00:40:49.330 --> 00:40:51.645
- Stay tuned.
- Stay tuned.

00:40:51.645 --> 00:40:54.478
(audience laughs)

00:40:56.650 --> 00:40:58.720
So we see evidence for quantifiers

00:40:58.720 --> 00:41:02.421
in language early in NSL,
which suggests that quantifiers

00:41:02.421 --> 00:41:04.990
could be a universal aspect of language

00:41:04.990 --> 00:41:07.843
that emerges early in a
newly developing language.

00:41:12.510 --> 00:41:14.310
I started my talk with the question

00:41:14.310 --> 00:41:16.500
where does language come from,

00:41:16.500 --> 00:41:18.430
and I hope that you've learned from this

00:41:18.430 --> 00:41:20.450
that the study of emerging sign languages

00:41:20.450 --> 00:41:22.853
really can help us address this question.

00:41:24.413 --> 00:41:27.879
This work demonstrated
several different things.

00:41:27.879 --> 00:41:31.810
First, humans are very well
equipped to create language,

00:41:31.810 --> 00:41:33.940
and the moment we have
the opportunity to do it,

00:41:33.940 --> 00:41:36.530
we do so and build a language.

00:41:36.530 --> 00:41:41.530
Secondly, full language does
not spring from a single child.

00:41:42.444 --> 00:41:46.099
There are many domains that require time

00:41:46.099 --> 00:41:48.490
or other structures to emerge,

00:41:48.490 --> 00:41:51.140
but it doesn't take hundreds
of thousands of years.

00:41:51.140 --> 00:41:52.990
Otherwise, we wouldn't see them here.

00:41:55.440 --> 00:42:00.340
Thirdly, the situation in
Nicaragua is rare and important,

00:42:00.340 --> 00:42:02.060
and we have an opportunity to see

00:42:02.060 --> 00:42:04.343
the emergence of a language in real time.

00:42:06.720 --> 00:42:09.400
That language occurred when deaf children

00:42:09.400 --> 00:42:11.433
were brought together in a school.

00:42:13.200 --> 00:42:14.360
What's interesting is that

00:42:14.360 --> 00:42:16.670
that situation is not that unique.

00:42:16.670 --> 00:42:20.223
Many sign languages around the
world developed in that way.

00:42:21.450 --> 00:42:25.480
So in the next talk by Dr. Amber Martin,

00:42:25.480 --> 00:42:27.450
she'll be discussing some of the effects

00:42:27.450 --> 00:42:29.790
of language deprivation.

00:42:29.790 --> 00:42:31.710
I wanna highlight here

00:42:31.710 --> 00:42:35.274
that children are
capable of amazing things

00:42:35.274 --> 00:42:37.433
if you just give them the opportunity.

00:42:38.382 --> 00:42:40.158
Thank you.

00:42:40.158 --> 00:42:43.158
(audience applause)

00:42:45.090 --> 00:42:46.619
Thank you all for coming.

00:42:46.619 --> 00:42:49.640
Thank you to my participants,

00:42:49.640 --> 00:42:51.803
my funders, and my collaborators.

00:42:56.750 --> 00:42:59.453
- So we have about 10
minutes for questions.

00:43:02.100 --> 00:43:04.780
And we have microphones,
so raise your hand

00:43:04.780 --> 00:43:07.143
and we will bring you the microphones.

00:43:12.350 --> 00:43:13.846
Excellent.

00:43:13.846 --> 00:43:15.918
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

00:43:15.918 --> 00:43:17.540
- [Male] Thank you, that was wonderful.

00:43:17.540 --> 00:43:19.760
I wanted to go back to something

00:43:19.760 --> 00:43:22.040
that you were pointing out towards the end

00:43:22.040 --> 00:43:24.013
in the quantifier study.

00:43:25.400 --> 00:43:28.896
As you know a lot of psycholinguistic work

00:43:28.896 --> 00:43:33.896
has tended to show that
in truth value tasks,

00:43:34.130 --> 00:43:37.160
children tend to be more logical,

00:43:37.160 --> 00:43:39.160
as Nowak and his colleagues put it,

00:43:39.160 --> 00:43:41.800
than adults in the sense that

00:43:41.800 --> 00:43:46.800
children are more likely
to judge sentences true

00:43:47.631 --> 00:43:51.480
if they have the form
some of the boys left

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:54.130
even though all of them did,

00:43:54.130 --> 00:43:57.480
and adults are much more likely to take

00:43:57.480 --> 00:44:01.710
the upper bounding implicature
into consideration,

00:44:01.710 --> 00:44:04.330
interpreting some as some but not all,

00:44:04.330 --> 00:44:06.970
and therefore rejecting sentences like

00:44:06.970 --> 00:44:09.900
some of the boys left if all of them did.

00:44:09.900 --> 00:44:14.664
So in a sense, as the Cohort
1 turns into Cohort 2,

00:44:14.664 --> 00:44:19.664
we have a case of ontogeny
recapitulating phylogeny

00:44:19.980 --> 00:44:23.180
in the sense that the
development of the language

00:44:23.180 --> 00:44:26.283
mirrors the development
within individuals.

00:44:28.930 --> 00:44:29.910
- [Interpreter] That's very true,

00:44:29.910 --> 00:44:31.460
and that's something
that we've thought about.

00:44:31.460 --> 00:44:34.320
One thing that I would point
out that's a bit different

00:44:34.320 --> 00:44:38.880
is that typically these children
comprehend the, don't...

00:44:39.978 --> 00:44:43.520
The tasks with children
are comprehension tasks,

00:44:43.520 --> 00:44:46.340
but typically what they produce sentences,

00:44:46.340 --> 00:44:48.920
they're not using
under-informative messages.

00:44:48.920 --> 00:44:52.510
They tend to express all, and
that's different from this.

00:44:52.510 --> 00:44:54.280
These are production tasks.

00:44:54.280 --> 00:44:56.900
So there does seem to be a parallel,

00:44:56.900 --> 00:45:01.560
but there's something different
going on here potentially.

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:02.910
- [Male] Thank you, so parallel a bit

00:45:02.910 --> 00:45:04.920
across different modalities then

00:45:04.920 --> 00:45:06.830
of production versus comprehension.

00:45:06.830 --> 00:45:07.663
Thank you.

00:45:16.670 --> 00:45:19.150
- [Woman] Hi, that was a great talk.

00:45:19.150 --> 00:45:22.250
So we know in the early
work by Senghas and Coppola

00:45:22.250 --> 00:45:27.198
that NSL signers used to
use paratactic structures

00:45:27.198 --> 00:45:30.003
for two animate arguments,

00:45:30.003 --> 00:45:33.054
such things like woman push, man fall,

00:45:33.054 --> 00:45:35.453
and people have interpreted it as

00:45:35.453 --> 00:45:38.740
that transitivity hadn't
emerged yet in the language,

00:45:38.740 --> 00:45:40.990
and that they prefer to
use paratactic structures.

00:45:40.990 --> 00:45:43.040
I don't know if you have any ideas about

00:45:43.040 --> 00:45:45.580
why there seems to be like discrepancy

00:45:45.580 --> 00:45:47.450
between being able to embed

00:45:47.450 --> 00:45:49.650
with like relative clauses
across all cohorts,

00:45:49.650 --> 00:45:51.830
but there was this earlier finding about

00:45:51.830 --> 00:45:55.170
not being able to have
two animate arguments

00:45:55.170 --> 00:45:56.513
in the single predicate.

00:45:59.930 --> 00:46:01.420
- [Interpreter] I wanna
modify a little bit.

00:46:01.420 --> 00:46:02.390
So they didn't say

00:46:02.390 --> 00:46:04.690
that they can't express
transitive sentences,

00:46:04.690 --> 00:46:08.230
it's that when there are
two animate arguments,

00:46:08.230 --> 00:46:11.943
it seems to be that each
argument gets its own verb.

00:46:13.090 --> 00:46:15.503
So man push, woman be pushed.

00:46:16.423 --> 00:46:20.653
And that's a perfectly fine description,

00:46:22.110 --> 00:46:24.150
so the argument expression...

00:46:25.160 --> 00:46:26.010
Wait, wait, wait.

00:46:29.070 --> 00:46:31.753
Does that match your
understanding of their work?

00:46:33.450 --> 00:46:34.730
I wouldn't say that they don't have

00:46:34.730 --> 00:46:37.553
the capacity to express
transitive sentences.

00:46:40.820 --> 00:46:43.130
- [Woman] I don't really
have a stance on them,

00:46:43.130 --> 00:46:45.250
I just know that that's
one of the interpretations

00:46:45.250 --> 00:46:47.550
of their findings from,
I think, other people.

00:46:51.570 --> 00:46:53.570
- [Interpreter] I think the question of,

00:46:55.700 --> 00:46:57.927
why do things appear early,
why do they come up early

00:46:57.927 --> 00:47:00.240
and other things come up later?

00:47:00.240 --> 00:47:02.780
Right now, we don't know enough,

00:47:02.780 --> 00:47:04.760
and I think this is work that will show

00:47:04.760 --> 00:47:07.530
where we study different domains

00:47:07.530 --> 00:47:10.840
to understand how the
language structure emerges

00:47:10.840 --> 00:47:12.720
and give it some comparison studies

00:47:12.720 --> 00:47:14.720
between home signers and NSL signers,

00:47:14.720 --> 00:47:18.160
but they can express transitive sentences,

00:47:18.160 --> 00:47:18.993
so I wanna make sure that

00:47:18.993 --> 00:47:20.770
that important point is made clearly.

00:47:20.770 --> 00:47:21.830
- [Woman] Thank you.

00:47:21.830 --> 00:47:23.133
I just have one more.

00:47:24.660 --> 00:47:28.300
So in your slides you
mentioned spatial agreements

00:47:28.300 --> 00:47:31.710
and using space for grammar
by the third cohort,

00:47:31.710 --> 00:47:34.140
but you didn't include person agreement.

00:47:34.140 --> 00:47:35.933
Is there any specific reason why?

00:47:38.380 --> 00:47:40.170
- [Interpreter] Because we
haven't researched that yet.

00:47:40.170 --> 00:47:42.240
- [Woman] Okay, (laughter) thank you.

00:47:42.240 --> 00:47:43.530
- [Interpreter] But that's a great area

00:47:43.530 --> 00:47:45.183
for future research, absolutely.

00:47:46.200 --> 00:47:48.800
And actually I have a study

00:47:48.800 --> 00:47:51.690
that is going in that
direction at some point,

00:47:51.690 --> 00:47:53.990
but yes, person agreement,
that's interesting.

00:48:02.180 --> 00:48:04.993
- There's a question
over here to the right.

00:48:09.980 --> 00:48:12.963
Oh, we need an interpreter
to voice her question.

00:48:14.952 --> 00:48:15.980
- [Woman] No.

00:48:15.980 --> 00:48:17.400
- No?
- But if it's easier,

00:48:17.400 --> 00:48:18.317
I can sign.

00:48:21.840 --> 00:48:24.083
Okay, sorry.

00:48:25.708 --> 00:48:28.430
I was just looking at your slide

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:31.730
about quantitative qualifiers,

00:48:31.730 --> 00:48:36.730
and on universal for the third cohort,

00:48:38.204 --> 00:48:43.204
for the second cohort, you see
them entirely using universal

00:48:43.370 --> 00:48:47.875
when universal is what's targeted.

00:48:47.875 --> 00:48:52.875
However, for the third cohort,
some go back to existential,

00:48:53.620 --> 00:48:54.930
and I was wondering if you had a theory

00:48:54.930 --> 00:48:56.130
as to why that might be.

00:48:57.970 --> 00:48:59.370
- [Interpreter] I suspect that's probably

00:48:59.370 --> 00:49:01.103
just a measurement error.

00:49:02.140 --> 00:49:05.100
I think we may have less
signers in that cohort,

00:49:05.100 --> 00:49:07.470
maybe, I'd have to look
more closely at it,

00:49:07.470 --> 00:49:08.720
but it's possible that when this,

00:49:08.720 --> 00:49:10.920
the signer was describing another picture.

00:49:10.920 --> 00:49:13.570
But at this point, I'm not
likely inclined to think

00:49:13.570 --> 00:49:17.090
that that's a meaningful difference.

00:49:17.090 --> 00:49:20.060
But if it is, then of course

00:49:20.060 --> 00:49:21.260
that'd be something to look into,

00:49:21.260 --> 00:49:22.823
but I suspect that it's not.

00:49:24.070 --> 00:49:27.560
Good question though, thank
you for asking about that.

00:49:27.560 --> 00:49:28.410
- [Woman] Thanks.

00:49:40.540 --> 00:49:42.860
- [Woman] Thank you very much,
this is very interesting.

00:49:42.860 --> 00:49:47.780
I had a question about the
relative clause findings

00:49:47.780 --> 00:49:51.730
where you showed that there
was a significant difference

00:49:51.730 --> 00:49:53.320
in the length of the verb.

00:49:53.320 --> 00:49:55.870
But you had also mentioned,
and it was evident in the video

00:49:55.870 --> 00:49:58.407
that in the embedded context,

00:49:58.407 --> 00:50:02.679
the movement of the verb
was repeated fewer times,

00:50:02.679 --> 00:50:04.520
which makes sense.

00:50:04.520 --> 00:50:07.220
You need a longer time to
have more movements in there.

00:50:07.220 --> 00:50:09.380
But I wondered if you had coded

00:50:09.380 --> 00:50:11.000
for anything other than length,

00:50:11.000 --> 00:50:13.870
and whether there were
other findings as well

00:50:13.870 --> 00:50:15.903
that were not significant.

00:50:20.030 --> 00:50:22.280
- [Interpreter] No, we
coded only for length.

00:50:22.280 --> 00:50:23.320
And...

00:50:25.560 --> 00:50:27.910
I'm not sure that I meant to say

00:50:29.390 --> 00:50:32.023
that the movement of the
sign itself was less.

00:50:34.240 --> 00:50:35.910
The sign happens more quickly

00:50:36.940 --> 00:50:40.720
so rather than some saying eat
apple with a larger movement,

00:50:40.720 --> 00:50:41.860
it was as much smaller.

00:50:41.860 --> 00:50:43.910
It could be that there's the
same number of movements,

00:50:43.910 --> 00:50:48.680
it's just that it's there's
less distance involved

00:50:48.680 --> 00:50:50.913
and it's faster to complete.

00:50:52.240 --> 00:50:54.960
One thing that we plan to do,
but have not yet looked at,

00:50:54.960 --> 00:50:58.870
is to take a look at
the non-manual markers

00:50:58.870 --> 00:51:01.340
so non-manual markers, for
those of you that don't know,

00:51:01.340 --> 00:51:03.300
are grammatical features of sign languages

00:51:03.300 --> 00:51:06.520
that tend to carry a lot of
information for it on the face.

00:51:06.520 --> 00:51:08.280
For example in American Sign Language we

00:51:08.280 --> 00:51:11.580
mark conditionals, like
if, with raised eyebrows.

00:51:11.580 --> 00:51:14.250
If it rains class will be canceled.

00:51:14.250 --> 00:51:16.800
And if there was no eyebrow raised,

00:51:16.800 --> 00:51:19.170
it would mean that it's a
declarative sentence instead.

00:51:19.170 --> 00:51:21.300
It rained and class is canceled.

00:51:21.300 --> 00:51:23.840
So we plan to take a look
at those kind of markers

00:51:23.840 --> 00:51:25.200
with this data.

00:51:25.200 --> 00:51:28.800
Because non-manual
markers tend to go along

00:51:28.800 --> 00:51:31.763
with relative clauses
in many sign languages.

00:51:33.220 --> 00:51:34.129
- [Woman] Thank you, that would be

00:51:34.129 --> 00:51:35.679
very interesting to hear about.

00:51:46.080 --> 00:51:49.490
- [Female] Hi, thank you
for coming and speaking.

00:51:49.490 --> 00:51:52.440
Please bear with me while I
try to formulate this question.

00:51:53.580 --> 00:51:54.605
- [Interpreter] Sure.

00:51:54.605 --> 00:51:57.470
- [Female] I noticed in
the slides trying to figure

00:51:57.470 --> 00:51:59.290
when I'm watching the different slides

00:51:59.290 --> 00:52:02.943
about the, was it bears swimming?

00:52:04.170 --> 00:52:05.180
- [Interpreter] Yeah the quantifiers, yes.

00:52:05.180 --> 00:52:06.310
- [Female] Thank you, right.

00:52:06.310 --> 00:52:07.860
What I noticed there were,

00:52:07.860 --> 00:52:10.400
one of the images showed the birds flying

00:52:10.400 --> 00:52:12.200
away from the tree.

00:52:12.200 --> 00:52:15.228
And I look at that and it feels causal,

00:52:15.228 --> 00:52:18.900
like something happened
to cause the birds to

00:52:18.900 --> 00:52:20.660
fly away from the tree.

00:52:20.660 --> 00:52:22.210
Now of course the same thing can be said,

00:52:22.210 --> 00:52:24.930
something happened to cause the boys

00:52:24.930 --> 00:52:26.680
to climb the tree and get the apples

00:52:26.680 --> 00:52:28.547
or to cause the bears,

00:52:28.547 --> 00:52:30.960
but it seems more

00:52:32.380 --> 00:52:34.932
naturally in an environment

00:52:34.932 --> 00:52:39.880
for a child, maybe, to
see birds fly from a tree.

00:52:39.880 --> 00:52:43.500
Did, this is where my bearing with me is

00:52:43.500 --> 00:52:45.250
a really great thing, so thank you.

00:52:46.240 --> 00:52:50.629
Did it at all, do you
think, effect the study

00:52:50.629 --> 00:52:55.629
the nature of those images about
birds flying from the tree,

00:52:56.830 --> 00:53:01.460
like a shotgun went off
or a car door slammed?

00:53:01.460 --> 00:53:04.200
So for me it feels more causal than

00:53:04.200 --> 00:53:06.783
the bears went swimming because XYZ.

00:53:06.783 --> 00:53:08.403
It's less causal.

00:53:10.850 --> 00:53:12.530
- [Interpreter] That's an
interesting observation.

00:53:12.530 --> 00:53:14.980
I think there's a few
things to say about that.

00:53:14.980 --> 00:53:18.693
One is that we didn't observe any kind of

00:53:18.693 --> 00:53:22.460
expressive language in
both the English speakers

00:53:22.460 --> 00:53:26.540
that we normed a study on or NSL signers.

00:53:26.540 --> 00:53:28.770
But suppose we had...

00:53:30.260 --> 00:53:33.110
Well it's not clear having a causal event

00:53:33.110 --> 00:53:35.160
would affect quantifier structure

00:53:35.160 --> 00:53:38.310
because it still shows the difference

00:53:38.310 --> 00:53:41.228
even if something caused
the birds to fly away,

00:53:41.228 --> 00:53:43.110
there wouldn't be a difference in the

00:53:43.110 --> 00:53:45.640
size of group of birds that flew away

00:53:45.640 --> 00:53:48.920
and what we were after was
that quantifier difference.

00:53:48.920 --> 00:53:52.350
Whether they wanted to
say some birds flew away

00:53:52.350 --> 00:53:53.883
or all of the birds flew away.

00:53:58.600 --> 00:54:03.000
- We have one minute left so
if you have a quick question,

00:54:03.000 --> 00:54:04.913
I think Nanyan has a question here.

00:54:09.040 --> 00:54:12.090
- [Nanyan] Oh, it may
not be a quick question.

00:54:12.090 --> 00:54:16.060
So I'm not very familiar with
the inflection system of ASL,

00:54:16.060 --> 00:54:20.603
but I was wondering so on the
topic of relative clauses,

00:54:20.603 --> 00:54:24.540
if we had a more elaborate description,

00:54:24.540 --> 00:54:29.540
say an action depicted by
even like with classifiers,

00:54:30.550 --> 00:54:34.094
what would happen in that situation?

00:54:34.094 --> 00:54:37.900
'Cause there's more motion involved,

00:54:37.900 --> 00:54:41.050
so in terms of the like reduced version

00:54:41.050 --> 00:54:43.983
and the relative clause,

00:54:44.840 --> 00:54:47.760
what are the things that will be reduced

00:54:47.760 --> 00:54:50.840
and how is that different from,

00:54:50.840 --> 00:54:55.840
say establishing an entity using space

00:54:55.910 --> 00:54:59.160
and then referring to
that entity in space?

00:54:59.160 --> 00:55:03.560
Would that be considered a paratactic use

00:55:03.560 --> 00:55:05.343
or a relative clause?

00:55:07.460 --> 00:55:09.440
- [Interpreter] Those
are all great questions

00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:12.020
and I'll try my best
to answer them quickly.

00:55:12.020 --> 00:55:14.600
In terms of whether we see
those kind of shortening

00:55:14.600 --> 00:55:18.010
with other kinds of structures
or classifiers or whatever,

00:55:18.010 --> 00:55:19.433
we plan to test that.

00:55:20.277 --> 00:55:24.700
We specifically did this
study only using action verbs

00:55:24.700 --> 00:55:27.430
that tended not to be using classifiers.

00:55:27.430 --> 00:55:29.610
'Cause classifiers, as
you know, are complicated.

00:55:29.610 --> 00:55:31.890
And they've got a lot of
things in them, not just verbs.

00:55:31.890 --> 00:55:34.150
They've got information about
the noun in them as well

00:55:34.150 --> 00:55:37.110
and some people have argued
that these are gestural or

00:55:37.110 --> 00:55:39.000
they're another linguistic structure.

00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:41.660
But your question is a good one and

00:55:41.660 --> 00:55:46.660
we do plan to study this to
figure out how far we push this.

00:55:46.660 --> 00:55:49.040
Is it only with verbs?

00:55:49.040 --> 00:55:52.233
Can it occur with double embedding?

00:55:54.853 --> 00:55:57.890
With subject and object linked?

00:55:57.890 --> 00:56:01.760
So all of those things
are future directions and

00:56:02.630 --> 00:56:03.740
you're on track with that.

00:56:03.740 --> 00:56:08.230
And your other question
was about pointing in space

00:56:08.230 --> 00:56:12.863
and if we would count that as parataxis?

00:56:14.520 --> 00:56:17.770
So the analysis of pointing

00:56:20.260 --> 00:56:23.340
is very complicated
and there's a lot of...

00:56:24.400 --> 00:56:26.900
there's not a lot of consensus

00:56:26.900 --> 00:56:30.380
in sign language literature
about what those mean

00:56:30.380 --> 00:56:31.213
in their analyses.

00:56:31.213 --> 00:56:33.220
But it's not about parataxis.

00:56:33.220 --> 00:56:35.450
It's definitely anaphoric.

00:56:35.450 --> 00:56:37.840
Often it refers back to something.

00:56:37.840 --> 00:56:41.493
But demonstratives, definates, pronouns,

00:56:43.330 --> 00:56:46.100
we don't know yet what
for sure what they are.

00:56:46.100 --> 00:56:47.460
- [Nanyan] Thank you so much.

00:56:47.460 --> 00:56:49.163
- Wonderful, thank you very much.

00:56:53.734 --> 00:56:58.401
Our second speaker will be
introduced by Jessica Tanner.

00:57:15.590 --> 00:57:17.350
- [Interpreter] Yay.

00:57:17.350 --> 00:57:18.880
Hello everyone, Hi.

00:57:18.880 --> 00:57:21.030
My name is Jessica Tanner

00:57:22.430 --> 00:57:23.763
and this is my name sign.

00:57:24.980 --> 00:57:27.100
It's so good to see so
many people here today.

00:57:27.100 --> 00:57:28.053
It's terrific.

00:57:31.670 --> 00:57:34.251
So, I just wanted to let you know

00:57:34.251 --> 00:57:39.010
that I am the first ASL
lecturer here at Yale University

00:57:39.010 --> 00:57:40.600
Yay, right?

00:57:40.600 --> 00:57:41.793
Bravo!

00:57:43.480 --> 00:57:46.940
So maybe, I'm not sure, but I
could be the first deaf lector

00:57:46.940 --> 00:57:48.520
here on faculty?

00:57:48.520 --> 00:57:50.240
I'm not sure, I might have to
do some more research on that

00:57:50.240 --> 00:57:51.770
and find out if I'm the first.

00:57:51.770 --> 00:57:53.380
But yeah, it's all good.

00:57:53.380 --> 00:57:55.820
So thank you so much to Yale University

00:57:55.820 --> 00:58:00.660
for successfully having
ASL for credit here.

00:58:00.660 --> 00:58:02.670
It's such a tremendous thing.

00:58:02.670 --> 00:58:05.623
So we want to keep that going,
so congratulations to Yale.

00:58:09.090 --> 00:58:10.330
It is my great honor here

00:58:10.330 --> 00:58:13.250
to introduce the second panelist today.

00:58:13.250 --> 00:58:18.250
Her name is Amber
Martin, Dr. Amber Martin.

00:58:21.370 --> 00:58:24.173
She has her Ph.D. in Psychology,

00:58:25.040 --> 00:58:27.643
and she's from the
University of Minnesota.

00:58:28.610 --> 00:58:31.563
And that's where she
received her Ph.D. in 2009.

00:58:33.450 --> 00:58:36.600
So now she's a professor

00:58:39.350 --> 00:58:43.890
of Developmental Psychology
from Hunter College.

00:58:43.890 --> 00:58:46.423
So that's really impressive, very nice.

00:58:48.030 --> 00:58:51.080
Her research is focused
on language acquisition

00:58:54.620 --> 00:58:56.940
and how it influences,

00:58:56.940 --> 00:59:00.133
how that language
deprivation has an influence.

00:59:01.020 --> 00:59:03.020
And her specific concentration...

00:59:05.620 --> 00:59:08.270
her specific concentration
is on language acquisition

00:59:09.305 --> 00:59:13.463
and a parallel with cognitive development.

00:59:16.230 --> 00:59:20.130
And she uses two different
languages for her research.

00:59:20.130 --> 00:59:21.620
She uses American Sign Language and

00:59:21.620 --> 00:59:24.450
Nicaraguan Sign Language
as well to study these.

00:59:24.450 --> 00:59:27.490
And she compares and analyzes how these,

00:59:27.490 --> 00:59:30.060
the interplay between these two languages,

00:59:30.060 --> 00:59:32.640
I mean how these two languages...

00:59:32.640 --> 00:59:34.180
She uses these two languages to study

00:59:34.180 --> 00:59:36.080
the interplay between these two ideas.

00:59:38.550 --> 00:59:42.390
And how this affects the
language development of children,

00:59:42.390 --> 00:59:44.790
of deaf children in particular.

00:59:44.790 --> 00:59:47.230
So I think this is a
really interesting topic.

00:59:47.230 --> 00:59:48.760
Are you all really
curious to find out what

00:59:48.760 --> 00:59:50.130
she's gonna speak about today?

00:59:50.130 --> 00:59:51.400
Yeah?

00:59:51.400 --> 00:59:55.230
So welcome to Dr. Amber Martin.

00:59:55.230 --> 00:59:57.365
Yay, thank you for coming.

00:59:57.365 --> 01:00:00.365
(audience applause)

01:00:54.550 --> 01:00:57.010
- [Interpreter] Good
afternoon, thank you so much.

01:00:57.010 --> 01:00:59.220
I'm honored to be here today

01:00:59.220 --> 01:01:02.590
and I very much appreciate the invitation

01:01:02.590 --> 01:01:04.830
and I welcome the opportunity

01:01:04.830 --> 01:01:06.453
to invite you all to my campus.

01:01:07.590 --> 01:01:08.950
As you just heard,

01:01:08.950 --> 01:01:12.550
Dr. Kocab talked about the
emergence of a new language

01:01:12.550 --> 01:01:14.600
and how it changed over time.

01:01:14.600 --> 01:01:16.880
The life of that language

01:01:18.320 --> 01:01:21.670
is a living, breathing, changing thing.

01:01:21.670 --> 01:01:25.160
Now I'm going to
transition to talking about

01:01:25.160 --> 01:01:28.160
language emergence and
growth in individuals.

01:01:28.160 --> 01:01:31.823
How individual children acquire language.

01:01:39.910 --> 01:01:43.310
As was previously mentioned,
my research looks at

01:01:43.310 --> 01:01:45.900
the effects of language deprivation

01:01:45.900 --> 01:01:47.823
and how that affects deaf children.

01:01:49.260 --> 01:01:53.340
What are the impacts of
early language acquisition

01:01:53.340 --> 01:01:56.293
and how does it affect
cognitive development?

01:02:01.780 --> 01:02:03.500
As mentioned in the introduction,

01:02:03.500 --> 01:02:06.040
my research centers on this question.

01:02:06.040 --> 01:02:09.713
How do language and
cognition shape each other?

01:02:14.550 --> 01:02:17.450
This is a very broad question, clearly.

01:02:17.450 --> 01:02:20.443
So the research could
go in many directions.

01:02:24.430 --> 01:02:26.840
One question I'm interested in

01:02:26.840 --> 01:02:31.840
is about which language you
have and when you learned it.

01:02:32.430 --> 01:02:36.150
How do those things affect
and shape you as a person?

01:02:36.150 --> 01:02:40.010
How do they shape the
user of the language?

01:02:40.010 --> 01:02:41.910
This is an interesting question to me.

01:02:48.900 --> 01:02:53.420
Here's another question, and
it really is full circle.

01:02:53.420 --> 01:02:55.310
What about the user?

01:02:55.310 --> 01:02:59.366
Do you, as an individual
language learner and user

01:02:59.366 --> 01:03:02.070
have an influence on the language itself,

01:03:02.070 --> 01:03:04.253
the language's developing structure?

01:03:05.150 --> 01:03:07.830
So it's a cyclical question.

01:03:07.830 --> 01:03:12.830
And there's an interplay here
with a bi-directional impact.

01:03:14.500 --> 01:03:16.430
I'm going to spend my time now

01:03:16.430 --> 01:03:18.920
talking about the first question.

01:03:18.920 --> 01:03:23.223
The language and how it
interacts with the children.

01:03:24.440 --> 01:03:28.773
But again, this question
does go bi-directionally.

01:03:37.900 --> 01:03:41.206
Many of you may have some knowledge

01:03:41.206 --> 01:03:43.810
about these statistics having to do with

01:03:43.810 --> 01:03:46.763
language experiences of deaf children.

01:03:48.540 --> 01:03:52.090
You may know that a very small
percentage of deaf children

01:03:52.090 --> 01:03:54.530
are born into an
environment where they have

01:03:54.530 --> 01:03:56.520
full access to a language because

01:03:56.520 --> 01:03:58.793
they have deaf parents
who use sign language.

01:04:00.600 --> 01:04:03.913
We call this group Deaf of Deaf or DOD.

01:04:06.780 --> 01:04:09.840
The rest of deaf children, 95 percent,

01:04:09.840 --> 01:04:11.640
experience a wide range of

01:04:11.640 --> 01:04:14.980
linguist experiences here
in the United States.

01:04:14.980 --> 01:04:17.853
There are some deaf children
born to hearing parents,

01:04:19.620 --> 01:04:23.293
typically who are not exposed
to sign language at birth.

01:04:27.000 --> 01:04:30.010
Hopefully that will change over time,

01:04:30.010 --> 01:04:35.010
but currently it is the state
that most hearing parents,

01:04:35.264 --> 01:04:37.820
when they have a deaf child,

01:04:37.820 --> 01:04:40.520
that's usually the first
deaf person they've ever met.

01:04:41.570 --> 01:04:44.690
Also among that 95
percent of deaf children,

01:04:44.690 --> 01:04:49.140
we see a very wide range
in language experiences.

01:04:49.140 --> 01:04:52.570
There are a variety of
intervention programs,

01:04:52.570 --> 01:04:54.160
educational approaches,

01:04:54.160 --> 01:04:56.850
some involving sign language, some not.

01:04:56.850 --> 01:04:59.530
Some focusing on speech only and

01:04:59.530 --> 01:05:01.610
sometimes they're paired
in different ways.

01:05:01.610 --> 01:05:03.800
Also there are various sign systems

01:05:03.800 --> 01:05:05.350
that children are introduced to.

01:05:05.350 --> 01:05:09.073
So it is a very wide-ranging dynamic.

01:05:22.630 --> 01:05:25.770
What that means for deaf children,

01:05:25.770 --> 01:05:28.400
in terms of early language acquisition

01:05:28.400 --> 01:05:30.590
and their early language experience,

01:05:30.590 --> 01:05:35.590
is that there are a variety of experiences

01:05:35.940 --> 01:05:39.470
and the quality of those
experiences differ.

01:05:39.470 --> 01:05:41.343
There's a great variation,

01:05:43.840 --> 01:05:45.330
and it depends on

01:05:45.330 --> 01:05:49.330
do they have good quality
access to language?

01:05:49.330 --> 01:05:53.240
That experience varies greatly.

01:05:53.240 --> 01:05:56.580
It depends often on the
quality of the input

01:05:56.580 --> 01:05:58.053
that a child is receiving.

01:05:59.940 --> 01:06:02.150
That applies not only to spoken language,

01:06:02.150 --> 01:06:03.550
but sign language as well.

01:06:03.550 --> 01:06:05.663
The quality of input can vary.

01:06:07.150 --> 01:06:09.910
And that quality of language input

01:06:10.920 --> 01:06:13.180
depends on the skill level of the adults

01:06:13.180 --> 01:06:14.230
in their environment.

01:06:15.910 --> 01:06:19.700
It also depends on factors
that are attitudinal

01:06:19.700 --> 01:06:24.173
about language exposure
for early children.

01:06:25.870 --> 01:06:27.763
That also varies greatly.

01:06:29.560 --> 01:06:33.980
A third factor that is
critical is time of exposure.

01:06:33.980 --> 01:06:37.490
The timing of a child's
first language experience

01:06:37.490 --> 01:06:40.870
and that varies greatly
among deaf children, as well.

01:06:40.870 --> 01:06:44.620
There are infants who are
exposed to language from birth

01:06:44.620 --> 01:06:46.870
whether it be a sign language or

01:06:46.870 --> 01:06:49.633
spoken language with
cochlear implant, at times.

01:06:51.000 --> 01:06:54.940
The first exposure to language,
that initial experience,

01:06:54.940 --> 01:06:57.210
sometimes happens far later.

01:06:57.210 --> 01:07:00.210
And from an early age to an older age,

01:07:00.210 --> 01:07:01.370
and everywhere in between,

01:07:01.370 --> 01:07:05.370
there is no profile of
the deaf student or child.

01:07:05.370 --> 01:07:06.890
It varies greatly.

01:07:06.890 --> 01:07:09.750
And that's been one
reason it's so difficult

01:07:09.750 --> 01:07:14.450
to study how do we
characterize deaf children

01:07:14.450 --> 01:07:16.403
and their language experience.

01:07:19.600 --> 01:07:24.600
This chart is about spoken
language trajectories

01:07:25.220 --> 01:07:28.400
for deaf children with cochlear implants.

01:07:28.400 --> 01:07:33.240
The gray area is children who are hearing

01:07:33.240 --> 01:07:34.820
and their trajectory.

01:07:34.820 --> 01:07:38.340
The rest of the lines
reflect deaf children.

01:07:38.340 --> 01:07:41.220
What this demonstrates is
that it's all over the map.

01:07:41.220 --> 01:07:44.360
Right, you see a wide variety.

01:07:44.360 --> 01:07:46.560
So there's a great deal
of variability here.

01:07:50.220 --> 01:07:55.220
That early language experience
is difficult to capture well.

01:08:04.040 --> 01:08:08.860
As a result, many deaf children experience

01:08:08.860 --> 01:08:11.163
what we call language deprivation.

01:08:15.550 --> 01:08:20.520
We don't actually have a
standard definition about

01:08:20.520 --> 01:08:24.623
what language deprivation
means, so here's a definition.

01:08:25.760 --> 01:08:29.340
It's true the experience
is extremely varied

01:08:29.340 --> 01:08:32.820
and it makes it very
difficult to characterize.

01:08:32.820 --> 01:08:36.810
Those who studied deaf children
do agree that deaf students

01:08:36.810 --> 01:08:41.330
often do experience some
degree of language deprivation.

01:08:41.330 --> 01:08:42.633
It's quite common.

01:08:45.040 --> 01:08:49.090
Language deprivation really means

01:08:49.090 --> 01:08:54.090
that a child was not exposed to language

01:08:55.174 --> 01:08:57.213
that was age-appropriate.

01:08:58.350 --> 01:09:03.070
And there's a great degree of
a lack of exposure to language

01:09:03.070 --> 01:09:05.643
during the critical
language learning period.

01:09:15.040 --> 01:09:18.470
So what I just described shows the picture

01:09:18.470 --> 01:09:21.060
of deaf children here in the United States

01:09:21.060 --> 01:09:24.380
and the variability in that experience.

01:09:24.380 --> 01:09:27.710
Now I'd like to talk about
the situation in Nicaragua,

01:09:27.710 --> 01:09:30.960
and the early language experience
for deaf children there.

01:09:30.960 --> 01:09:34.030
There are some similarities
between Nicaragua

01:09:34.030 --> 01:09:37.270
and the United States, in that
respect and some differences.

01:09:37.270 --> 01:09:39.130
Among the similarities are that

01:09:39.130 --> 01:09:43.390
most deaf children born in Nicaragua

01:09:43.390 --> 01:09:45.140
have parents who don't know sign language

01:09:45.140 --> 01:09:46.343
and who are not deaf.

01:09:48.590 --> 01:09:53.240
Also in Nicaragua, there is no standard.

01:09:53.240 --> 01:09:55.540
There are no standards
for early intervention.

01:09:56.520 --> 01:10:00.779
They don't have programs
to support parents

01:10:00.779 --> 01:10:05.563
after they have a diagnosis.

01:10:07.380 --> 01:10:11.130
In Nicaragua, the deaf children there,

01:10:11.130 --> 01:10:11.993
we know clearly,

01:10:14.890 --> 01:10:18.280
that their first language experience

01:10:18.280 --> 01:10:20.183
is not until they enter school.

01:10:21.210 --> 01:10:24.610
The age of onset of
language acquisition happens

01:10:24.610 --> 01:10:27.520
at the same time as
their entry into school.

01:10:27.520 --> 01:10:30.970
So the year or the day that they

01:10:30.970 --> 01:10:35.080
start being exposed to language
can be clearly identified

01:10:35.080 --> 01:10:37.000
because it's clearly indicated by

01:10:37.000 --> 01:10:38.900
the time at which they entered school.

01:10:42.230 --> 01:10:46.220
In addition, children who
come in to the school there

01:10:46.220 --> 01:10:48.720
are exposed to the same peer group

01:10:48.720 --> 01:10:50.990
and others in their environment,

01:10:50.990 --> 01:10:55.990
so they have a consistent
language exposure experience.

01:10:58.470 --> 01:11:01.580
So some of those early
language experiences are

01:11:01.580 --> 01:11:05.450
similar to those of students
in the United States,

01:11:05.450 --> 01:11:08.073
but there are some areas that are unique.

01:11:10.392 --> 01:11:13.393
This brings me to my research question:

01:11:16.700 --> 01:11:21.570
How are language and
cognition interdependent

01:11:23.140 --> 01:11:26.750
and what are the
consequences linguistically

01:11:26.750 --> 01:11:31.383
and cognitively of early
language deprivation?

01:11:44.720 --> 01:11:49.720
So just because of the
modality of signed languages,

01:11:52.730 --> 01:11:56.703
the spatial structure is very rich.

01:11:58.750 --> 01:12:03.750
We use space in sign
language to talk about space

01:12:04.470 --> 01:12:06.620
but for many other reasons, as well,

01:12:06.620 --> 01:12:09.383
and to express many types
of grammatical structures.

01:12:11.490 --> 01:12:13.900
So automatically we
see that sign languages

01:12:13.900 --> 01:12:16.810
are very rich in what we can do,

01:12:16.810 --> 01:12:20.793
in terms of spatial information
in studying these languages.

01:12:21.970 --> 01:12:23.680
we can talk about things that

01:12:23.680 --> 01:12:25.340
are physically in our environment

01:12:25.340 --> 01:12:30.340
and point to express that
information in shared space.

01:12:32.380 --> 01:12:36.803
We also use space for co-referencing.

01:12:38.660 --> 01:12:42.920
So if I want to talk about two
people, a teacher and a TA,

01:12:42.920 --> 01:12:46.600
I can establish those two
reference in a low sigh,

01:12:46.600 --> 01:12:49.453
to indicate the two different people.

01:12:51.930 --> 01:12:55.320
But it's not only used for
talking about real things.

01:12:55.320 --> 01:13:00.320
We can also use space to talk
about cognition or ideas.

01:13:00.380 --> 01:13:04.020
We can talk about comparing two ideas

01:13:04.020 --> 01:13:07.423
and how those two ideas are interrelated.

01:13:08.480 --> 01:13:11.440
We could talk about justice,
for example, here on my right

01:13:11.440 --> 01:13:13.303
and freedom on my left.

01:13:14.160 --> 01:13:18.103
And talk about how those two
things are related using space.

01:13:21.840 --> 01:13:25.820
Also, in the syntactic structure
of the language itself,

01:13:25.820 --> 01:13:29.540
in ASL there are rich uses of space

01:13:29.540 --> 01:13:31.290
and here are some examples of that.

01:13:33.180 --> 01:13:35.180
I can say here's the teacher

01:13:37.260 --> 01:13:41.000
and the teacher gave a paper to the TA

01:13:41.000 --> 01:13:43.530
to distribute among the students.

01:13:43.530 --> 01:13:47.010
The directionality tells
you who's doing what to whom

01:13:47.010 --> 01:13:51.603
and also modifications can be
done to the verb using space.

01:13:54.000 --> 01:13:59.000
That modality question is very rich

01:13:59.200 --> 01:14:01.350
because of the way signed languages

01:14:01.350 --> 01:14:03.613
make use of signing space.

01:14:11.620 --> 01:14:15.540
In Nicaraguan Sign Language,
there are some similarities

01:14:15.540 --> 01:14:17.880
in the way they use
space in their language

01:14:17.880 --> 01:14:20.970
and research has shown how

01:14:20.970 --> 01:14:24.590
emergence of spatial modulation

01:14:25.820 --> 01:14:30.003
has appeared in Nicaraguan
Sign Language for verbs.

01:14:31.710 --> 01:14:34.120
I have a few things I want to show you

01:14:34.120 --> 01:14:36.860
that aren't specific to Nicaragua but

01:14:36.860 --> 01:14:38.473
other sign languages as well.

01:14:39.650 --> 01:14:44.520
The way use of space is
organized can be horizontally,

01:14:44.520 --> 01:14:47.040
where we tend to place things in space

01:14:47.040 --> 01:14:49.190
across a horizontal plane.

01:14:49.190 --> 01:14:52.160
You don't have a person
up above and down below.

01:14:52.160 --> 01:14:55.550
In theory we could use a vertical plane

01:14:55.550 --> 01:14:57.330
in which to organize that,

01:14:57.330 --> 01:14:59.350
but typically that's
not the way it's done.

01:14:59.350 --> 01:15:01.580
It's the horizontal plane.

01:15:01.580 --> 01:15:04.780
But we also do that
for different purposes.

01:15:04.780 --> 01:15:07.970
We can use a vertical
organization to talk about things

01:15:07.970 --> 01:15:11.430
like relative states,
or a relative status,

01:15:11.430 --> 01:15:12.833
excuse me, of people.

01:15:14.480 --> 01:15:17.723
So the horizontal plane
is very commonly used.

01:15:30.570 --> 01:15:33.400
So the modality-specific considerations

01:15:33.400 --> 01:15:35.330
give us an opportunity to explore

01:15:35.330 --> 01:15:38.410
the relationship between
the use of space and

01:15:38.410 --> 01:15:42.453
non-linguistic cognitive spatial skills.

01:15:44.830 --> 01:15:47.810
And this isn't new research,
it's been around for a while

01:15:47.810 --> 01:15:49.450
looking at this question but

01:15:49.450 --> 01:15:52.830
it's a great place to start
because again, of the richness.

01:15:52.830 --> 01:15:56.090
of the way signed languages use space.

01:15:56.090 --> 01:15:58.320
Different studies have in fact shown

01:15:58.320 --> 01:16:02.653
that signers use mental rotation.

01:16:04.470 --> 01:16:07.480
Emmorey looked at this in prior research

01:16:07.480 --> 01:16:09.483
and my work has as well.

01:16:11.140 --> 01:16:13.660
When we see people who use sign language

01:16:13.660 --> 01:16:15.590
who are acquiring sign language,

01:16:15.590 --> 01:16:20.590
we look at their ability to
mentally rotate the formations,

01:16:21.150 --> 01:16:25.633
and, that's transformations,
in the linguistic domain.

01:16:34.460 --> 01:16:37.100
So now I'd like to look at this question.

01:16:37.100 --> 01:16:40.693
Let's go back to the importance
of age of acquisition.

01:16:43.582 --> 01:16:46.870
Does that impact or does that relationship

01:16:46.870 --> 01:16:48.770
between language and cognition

01:16:49.760 --> 01:16:53.633
depend on the age at which
a child acquires language?

01:16:59.080 --> 01:17:02.500
And when do those benefits appear?

01:17:02.500 --> 01:17:07.130
Do they appear early or does
it not happen until later?

01:17:07.130 --> 01:17:09.193
At what age do they emerge?

01:17:12.160 --> 01:17:16.470
Also, when we talk about benefits

01:17:16.470 --> 01:17:20.283
and the use of space and
spatial cognitive skills,

01:17:22.790 --> 01:17:27.410
are there specific devices that
are used in sign languages?

01:17:27.410 --> 01:17:29.240
How general are they and

01:17:29.240 --> 01:17:32.789
how specific are those
benefits that are carried

01:17:32.789 --> 01:17:37.613
from the language over to cognition?

01:17:44.890 --> 01:17:47.970
We wanted to develop
a mental rotation task

01:17:47.970 --> 01:17:49.563
to look at these questions.

01:17:50.770 --> 01:17:54.823
We developed tasks using computers.

01:17:56.260 --> 01:17:58.930
I'll explain what happens in the tests

01:17:58.930 --> 01:18:02.163
and show you an example of
someone actually taking the test.

01:18:05.360 --> 01:18:08.300
These shapes represent what
a participant would see

01:18:08.300 --> 01:18:09.430
on a computer screen.

01:18:09.430 --> 01:18:12.100
So they're presented with an image

01:18:13.190 --> 01:18:15.340
and then they have the real object

01:18:15.340 --> 01:18:17.560
that they're physically holding.

01:18:17.560 --> 01:18:19.700
And they look at two pictures

01:18:19.700 --> 01:18:22.440
and they have to determine
which one matches

01:18:22.440 --> 01:18:24.853
the actual physical
object in front of them.

01:18:27.570 --> 01:18:29.710
And it's a touchscreen so they can respond

01:18:29.710 --> 01:18:31.120
by touching the screen.

01:18:31.120 --> 01:18:35.580
We measure response time, we
can also measure accuracy.

01:18:35.580 --> 01:18:39.253
How quickly can they make those decisions?

01:18:44.780 --> 01:18:49.110
Importantly, we are comparing
several different aspects of

01:18:49.110 --> 01:18:51.300
the mental rotation task.

01:18:51.300 --> 01:18:54.800
One is we're looking at the plane used

01:18:55.920 --> 01:18:57.743
to compare the mental rotation.

01:18:59.230 --> 01:19:04.230
Is it a horizontal plane or
is it on a different plane?

01:19:04.750 --> 01:19:07.803
For example if you're thinking
about a physical table,

01:19:09.500 --> 01:19:13.310
like maybe a Lazy Susan, when
you think about that rotation

01:19:13.310 --> 01:19:17.463
on a horizontal plane,
can you mentally represent

01:19:17.463 --> 01:19:20.836
both sides of that rotation

01:19:20.836 --> 01:19:25.630
and can you modulate that
visually in your mind.

01:19:25.630 --> 01:19:29.460
Or similarly, a picture moving on a wall

01:19:30.570 --> 01:19:32.520
along two different planes of rotation.

01:19:33.840 --> 01:19:37.020
And also we use two
different types of objects,

01:19:37.020 --> 01:19:40.263
a simple shape and a human figure.

01:19:41.210 --> 01:19:45.090
So we wanted to look
at two different areas

01:19:46.070 --> 01:19:51.070
where are specific effects seen

01:19:51.570 --> 01:19:54.230
with users of sign language
when using an object

01:19:54.230 --> 01:19:55.493
versus as a human figure.

01:19:58.390 --> 01:20:00.433
This is an example of the task.

01:20:30.370 --> 01:20:35.200
For part of our analysis, we looked at

01:20:35.200 --> 01:20:38.470
the people who had early
exposure to language

01:20:39.710 --> 01:20:44.710
and we wanted to look at
ASL signers in this respect.

01:20:46.660 --> 01:20:51.160
Signers of ASL, native signers of ASL.

01:20:51.160 --> 01:20:55.280
We also compared them to
children who learned later,

01:20:55.280 --> 01:20:59.080
at the age of three or six.

01:20:59.080 --> 01:21:02.883
And we compared them also
to hearing non-signers.

01:21:08.120 --> 01:21:10.410
What we found is reflected in this graph.

01:21:10.410 --> 01:21:15.410
Those who learn language
earlier are better at accuracy.

01:21:16.520 --> 01:21:20.123
So the higher bars
represent greater accuracy.

01:21:22.040 --> 01:21:25.030
The early learners
overall performed better

01:21:26.030 --> 01:21:28.513
than those who learned at a later age.

01:21:37.580 --> 01:21:42.283
We also found effects
of the plane rotation.

01:21:46.060 --> 01:21:51.060
Overall, signers did
better with rotation tasks

01:21:51.187 --> 01:21:53.963
that were horizontal than vertical.

01:21:55.100 --> 01:21:58.900
Also, hearing people did better

01:21:58.900 --> 01:22:02.690
with horizontal than
vertical rotation tasks.

01:22:02.690 --> 01:22:05.263
Overall, that was generally true.

01:22:10.760 --> 01:22:13.500
Oh I'm sorry, let me go back.

01:22:13.500 --> 01:22:17.390
You'll also notice here
that early language learners

01:22:18.570 --> 01:22:21.380
we see no difference between vertical

01:22:21.380 --> 01:22:23.660
and horizontal plane rotation.

01:22:23.660 --> 01:22:25.093
Those results are the same.

01:22:26.510 --> 01:22:30.930
But the late learners do
better with horizontal

01:22:30.930 --> 01:22:33.260
and not as well with vertical,

01:22:33.260 --> 01:22:37.413
which mirrors the results
with their hearing peers.

01:22:46.930 --> 01:22:51.100
Also, all of the groups did
better with human figures

01:22:52.937 --> 01:22:56.640
than the block task,
which was interesting.

01:22:56.640 --> 01:23:00.480
Something about the shape
versus the human figure.

01:23:00.480 --> 01:23:03.743
That result was consistent
among all groups.

01:23:09.810 --> 01:23:11.610
What does this demonstrate?

01:23:11.610 --> 01:23:13.320
It demonstrates the importance

01:23:13.320 --> 01:23:17.763
of early language acquisition
on mental rotation skills.

01:23:19.420 --> 01:23:23.320
So language and cognition
are influenced by

01:23:23.320 --> 01:23:25.560
the age of language acquisition.

01:23:25.560 --> 01:23:27.420
Those who learn earlier perform better

01:23:27.420 --> 01:23:30.140
than those who learn later,

01:23:30.140 --> 01:23:34.800
but the deaf children who
learned sign language later

01:23:34.800 --> 01:23:37.310
still perform better on these tasks than

01:23:37.310 --> 01:23:38.883
the hearing non-signers.

01:23:48.840 --> 01:23:53.143
Now to bring this back to

01:23:53.143 --> 01:23:56.300
the equation that we
originally talked about,

01:23:56.300 --> 01:24:00.263
what changes over time
as a language emerges?

01:24:03.090 --> 01:24:06.696
And are there differences across cohorts

01:24:06.696 --> 01:24:09.373
in their use of space?

01:24:17.260 --> 01:24:21.490
Here we looked across cohorts of signers

01:24:21.490 --> 01:24:23.963
and we also tested hearing people.

01:24:26.030 --> 01:24:28.040
The hearing people were included

01:24:28.040 --> 01:24:32.260
for the mental rotation
task but not language usage,

01:24:32.260 --> 01:24:34.060
in terms of sign language obviously.

01:24:39.740 --> 01:24:41.053
These are the results.

01:24:50.410 --> 01:24:55.410
Okay, so here you see
the change over time.

01:24:55.620 --> 01:24:59.420
I'm sorry, this is the
mental rotation task.

01:24:59.420 --> 01:25:02.570
We see improvement
across cohorts over time

01:25:03.750 --> 01:25:06.480
with each successive
cohort performing better

01:25:06.480 --> 01:25:07.733
than the one before.

01:25:11.630 --> 01:25:16.430
Also we see that all
cohorts perform better

01:25:16.430 --> 01:25:17.963
than the hearing group.

01:25:19.020 --> 01:25:20.730
Here we see a different pattern.

01:25:20.730 --> 01:25:24.450
It depends on the type of rotation task

01:25:24.450 --> 01:25:25.793
that's being performed.

01:25:27.580 --> 01:25:29.913
With the human figure task,

01:25:31.230 --> 01:25:36.230
you'll notice the solid bars reflect this

01:25:38.084 --> 01:25:43.084
and performance improvement
is observed immediately.

01:25:46.630 --> 01:25:50.540
Cohort 1 signers do much
better than the hearing group

01:25:51.520 --> 01:25:54.770
in mental rotation tasks
involving human figures.

01:25:54.770 --> 01:25:57.580
So that happens very, very quickly.

01:25:57.580 --> 01:26:00.600
For the blocked task,
that's a simple object

01:26:00.600 --> 01:26:03.900
not a human figure, we
see that it takes longer

01:26:03.900 --> 01:26:06.633
for that improvement to
take place over time.

01:26:09.030 --> 01:26:12.030
We see a different pattern of

01:26:12.030 --> 01:26:13.700
mental rotation skill improvement,

01:26:13.700 --> 01:26:18.150
depending on what type of
mental rotation is involved,

01:26:18.150 --> 01:26:19.793
the block or the human figure.

01:26:23.470 --> 01:26:28.455
Also, the time at which
a child enters school

01:26:28.455 --> 01:26:32.163
can impact their mental rotation capacity.

01:26:36.720 --> 01:26:40.393
So now let's look at what
changes in a language.

01:26:42.240 --> 01:26:43.980
Some research has been done about

01:26:43.980 --> 01:26:48.740
various types of space
used in sign languages.

01:26:48.740 --> 01:26:51.410
Here we looked at one particular type

01:26:51.410 --> 01:26:54.560
of space usage that changes over time.

01:26:54.560 --> 01:26:58.300
So there's the x-axis that's
used for verb movement

01:27:00.980 --> 01:27:02.930
and if a signer wants to describe,

01:27:02.930 --> 01:27:05.163
for example who's doing what to whom,

01:27:06.882 --> 01:27:09.410
for example giving of a book,

01:27:09.410 --> 01:27:13.030
you could use this axis where
it originates from the signer.

01:27:13.030 --> 01:27:18.030
I'm the person and the give
motion goes outward from my body

01:27:18.480 --> 01:27:20.130
across the signing space.

01:27:20.130 --> 01:27:22.993
That's what we refer to as the z-axis.

01:27:25.190 --> 01:27:27.938
Or you could have the verb moving

01:27:27.938 --> 01:27:31.850
across the x-axis from right to left

01:27:31.850 --> 01:27:33.720
in the space in front of me,

01:27:33.720 --> 01:27:36.150
where I establish to reference and

01:27:36.150 --> 01:27:38.060
the give goes horizontally.

01:27:38.060 --> 01:27:42.140
So the x-axis or the z-axis can be used.

01:27:42.140 --> 01:27:44.630
There's been research and other
types of sign languages too,

01:27:44.630 --> 01:27:49.630
that use the z-axis very early on

01:27:50.710 --> 01:27:52.040
in the emergence of the language.

01:27:52.040 --> 01:27:55.910
There are other languages
where it seems to emerge later.

01:27:55.910 --> 01:27:58.863
So we wanted to look at
this emergence in NSL.

01:28:00.790 --> 01:28:05.480
What we did was ask people
to sign different situations.

01:28:05.480 --> 01:28:09.290
The stimuli involved punching actions

01:28:09.290 --> 01:28:11.530
and various types of verbs.

01:28:11.530 --> 01:28:13.090
So let me show you an example

01:28:13.090 --> 01:28:15.183
of what the signing language looks like.

01:28:25.550 --> 01:28:30.103
So you notice the clear
z-axis in use here.

01:28:41.080 --> 01:28:45.320
And here you see of that horizontal axis

01:28:45.320 --> 01:28:49.253
with body shifting using the x-axis.

01:28:54.270 --> 01:28:59.270
The use of the x-axis emerged more slowly

01:28:59.470 --> 01:29:01.200
across the cohorts.

01:29:01.200 --> 01:29:03.690
Cohort 1 signers do use it,

01:29:03.690 --> 01:29:08.240
but we see increased usage
across Cohorts 1, 2, and 3.

01:29:08.240 --> 01:29:10.700
By Cohort 3, the signers are using that

01:29:10.700 --> 01:29:11.853
much more frequently.

01:29:17.560 --> 01:29:18.783
Now lastly,

01:29:21.310 --> 01:29:26.310
I'd like to describe what this data shows

01:29:26.370 --> 01:29:30.080
and just see if you can
observe the use of x-axis,

01:29:30.080 --> 01:29:31.670
as well as how that correlates

01:29:31.670 --> 01:29:34.053
with mental rotation task performance.

01:29:35.550 --> 01:29:39.250
And I don't have a
statistical analysis but

01:29:39.250 --> 01:29:41.530
I want you to pay attention
to those who perform well

01:29:41.530 --> 01:29:44.470
on mental rotation tasks
versus those who don't

01:29:44.470 --> 01:29:49.330
and who tends to use the
x-axis and who does not.

01:29:49.330 --> 01:29:53.220
This graph shows that
people who tend to do well

01:29:53.220 --> 01:29:58.193
on mental rotation also use the x-axis.

01:29:59.430 --> 01:30:01.083
As you can see in these bars.

01:30:02.240 --> 01:30:05.750
Those who don't perform as
well on mental rotation tasks

01:30:05.750 --> 01:30:09.080
also don't typically use the x-axis.

01:30:09.080 --> 01:30:12.763
So we see that those two
performances hang together.

01:30:14.970 --> 01:30:17.100
And this is not concrete evidence

01:30:17.100 --> 01:30:19.080
for a causal relationship here,

01:30:19.080 --> 01:30:21.460
but it is something that we,

01:30:21.460 --> 01:30:23.713
it is consistent with that possibility.

01:30:32.000 --> 01:30:37.000
So what do we think is
happening here with this cycle?

01:30:37.170 --> 01:30:40.822
How does spatial language
an entry into access

01:30:40.822 --> 01:30:45.822
to that language carry over
to skills of spatial cognition

01:30:46.770 --> 01:30:49.003
and then the interplay, yet again,

01:30:49.003 --> 01:30:52.740
with the development of the language

01:30:52.740 --> 01:30:55.670
and the interplay
between those two things?

01:30:55.670 --> 01:30:57.883
What we see is this trend,

01:30:58.740 --> 01:31:02.053
the use of the z-axis emerges very early.

01:31:04.820 --> 01:31:08.140
Mental rotation skill also emerges early

01:31:09.240 --> 01:31:11.670
for the human figure, specifically,

01:31:11.670 --> 01:31:16.670
and then later we see the use
of the z-axis or the x-axis

01:31:18.150 --> 01:31:21.090
and then also mental rotation abilities

01:31:21.090 --> 01:31:23.423
for other objects, like the block.

01:31:26.010 --> 01:31:31.010
So we see bi-directionality
in these influences.

01:31:32.670 --> 01:31:37.000
These emergences and changes happen both

01:31:37.000 --> 01:31:39.530
when specific features enter the language

01:31:39.530 --> 01:31:43.240
and how those specific features feed

01:31:43.240 --> 01:31:45.860
cognitive changes in its users.

01:31:45.860 --> 01:31:47.580
Those cognitive changes, in turn,

01:31:47.580 --> 01:31:51.122
may feedback to affect the grammar,

01:31:51.122 --> 01:31:55.040
especially the spatial
aspects of the language.

01:31:55.040 --> 01:31:58.930
And what we see here is a
cycle of interdependence.

01:31:58.930 --> 01:32:02.112
We see both language and cognition

01:32:02.112 --> 01:32:06.660
are co-organized throughout
the languages development

01:32:06.660 --> 01:32:09.303
and within individual children.

01:32:14.460 --> 01:32:19.310
I'd like to thank all of the participants

01:32:19.310 --> 01:32:22.490
and those who supported my research work

01:32:22.490 --> 01:32:24.473
and thank you so much for having me.

01:32:26.379 --> 01:32:29.379
(audience applause)

01:32:36.840 --> 01:32:39.130
- Thank you for a wonderful talk.

01:32:39.130 --> 01:32:42.580
We have a couple of minutes for questions

01:32:42.580 --> 01:32:45.480
and then we're gonna break
for coffee and then come back.

01:32:45.480 --> 01:32:47.133
So just a question or two.

01:32:55.190 --> 01:32:56.533
Do I see questions?

01:32:57.550 --> 01:32:58.840
Yes.

01:32:58.840 --> 01:33:00.030
- [Female] Hi there.

01:33:00.030 --> 01:33:05.030
I just had a question about
students that learn ASL

01:33:05.450 --> 01:33:07.380
and if you did any
research on the difference

01:33:07.380 --> 01:33:10.720
if they were mainstreamed
into a public school

01:33:10.720 --> 01:33:13.100
or if they went to a School for the Deaf

01:33:13.100 --> 01:33:15.210
and their differences in

01:33:16.300 --> 01:33:19.373
the recognition of space and movement.

01:33:21.434 --> 01:33:22.673
- [Interpreter] Yes.

01:33:24.322 --> 01:33:29.020
I don't know if specific
research has looked at

01:33:32.796 --> 01:33:37.796
the content where children
acquire language or the context

01:33:42.080 --> 01:33:45.430
but in terms of, for example,

01:33:45.430 --> 01:33:48.230
mainstream schools versus
Schools for the Deaf,

01:33:48.230 --> 01:33:52.550
I don't know but certainly the
quality of the language input

01:33:52.550 --> 01:33:55.700
and the richness of that language input

01:33:56.570 --> 01:34:01.570
will contribute to spatial
and cognitive development.

01:34:01.620 --> 01:34:04.709
I don't know specific
research that looks at that

01:34:04.709 --> 01:34:07.663
in those different contexts educationally,

01:34:08.960 --> 01:34:10.163
So I don't know.

01:34:12.270 --> 01:34:13.570
- Okay. One more question.

01:34:17.090 --> 01:34:19.230
- [Female] I'm a grad student at Gallaudet

01:34:19.230 --> 01:34:21.580
with a Master's in Sign Language Education

01:34:21.580 --> 01:34:24.660
and I learned a lot here today.

01:34:24.660 --> 01:34:29.020
One thing that struck me as
new was the x- and y-axis

01:34:29.020 --> 01:34:32.653
and I'm wondering how
you came up with that.

01:34:34.670 --> 01:34:38.880
- [Interpreter] The idea of
looking at the x- and y-axes

01:34:39.840 --> 01:34:42.200
actually started with research

01:34:42.200 --> 01:34:44.920
in other sign languages like ABSL

01:34:46.228 --> 01:34:49.460
and other emerging sign
languages in the world.

01:34:49.460 --> 01:34:53.593
So what we wondered about
is, would the same be true.

01:34:54.860 --> 01:34:59.860
So Carol Padden and Mayer and
others were looking at ABSL.

01:35:02.900 --> 01:35:04.880
So we borrowed their ideas

01:35:04.880 --> 01:35:07.560
and applied it to Nicaraguan Sign Language

01:35:07.560 --> 01:35:09.460
to look for the same
patterns of development

01:35:09.460 --> 01:35:13.540
and in fact we did see

01:35:13.540 --> 01:35:17.640
more use of the x-axis over time in NSL

01:35:17.640 --> 01:35:21.473
and spatial organization
changed, too, across the cohorts.

01:35:24.185 --> 01:35:25.018
- Thank you.

01:35:26.156 --> 01:35:31.156
So we will have more time
for questions at the end

01:35:31.210 --> 01:35:32.610
when there is a panel discussion.

01:35:32.610 --> 01:35:34.820
So is that okay to hold on?

01:35:34.820 --> 01:35:37.520
Okay, so we're gonna
take a 15 minute break,

01:35:37.520 --> 01:35:39.970
no longer than that just to get up.

01:35:39.970 --> 01:35:42.010
We have some coffee and tea and pastries.

01:35:42.010 --> 01:35:43.820
And a restroom right there.

01:35:43.820 --> 01:35:46.080
So, get something to drink quickly

01:35:46.080 --> 01:35:47.342
and please come back.

01:35:47.342 --> 01:35:48.867
Okay, excellent.

01:35:48.867 --> 01:35:49.700
Thank you.

